/* AQA GCSE Mathematics (8300) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Sonnet 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'AQA-8300', KITS: {"3.1.1":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"3.1.1","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Number: Structure and calculation is the toolkit every other part of GCSE Mathematics depends on: you cannot solve an equation, simplify a ratio, work out a probability or read a graph without first being able to order values, apply the four operations accurately, follow the correct order of operations, and give an exact or appropriately rounded answer. It is examined without a calculator on Paper 1 and with a calculator on Papers 2 and 3, so both mental fluency and formal written method matter, and AQA weights Number at 25% of the marks at Foundation tier and 15% at Higher tier — joint highest at Foundation tier, level with Ratio, proportion and rates of change, at 25% — so the habits built in this room protect marks in every other room of the course too.","idea":[{"code":"N1","h":"Ordering numbers and the comparison symbols","t":"Order positive and negative integers, decimals and fractions by comparing them on a single scale, such as a number line, remembering that a negative number is smaller the further it is from zero. Convert fractions to decimals, or give every value a common denominator, before comparing them directly. This is basic foundation content, so every Foundation and Higher student is examined on it: the paper asks you to 'order … starting with the smallest' or to insert =, ≠, <, >, ≤ or ≥ between two expressions, and the mark scheme awards the mark only when every value, not just the extremes, is in the correct position."},{"code":"N2","h":"The four operations, formal written methods and place value","t":"Add, subtract, multiply and divide integers, decimals and fractions (including mixed numbers), using formal written methods such as column addition, long multiplication and long division, and understanding place value when numbers are very large, very small, or have several decimal places. Questions are set in context, including household finance terms such as profit, loss, cost price, selling price, debit, credit, balance, income tax, VAT and interest rate. This is basic foundation content examined on every paper; where a question says you must show your working, the mark scheme gives method marks for a correct formal method even if the final accuracy mark is lost to an arithmetic slip."},{"code":"N3","h":"Relationships between operations and priority of operations","t":"Recognise inverse operations — for example, that dividing undoes multiplying — to simplify a calculation or check an answer, such as spotting that 17 × 23 ÷ 23 needs no calculating at all. Use the conventional order of operations: brackets, then indices (powers, roots and reciprocals), then division and multiplication, then addition and subtraction. This is basic foundation content: a non-calculator 'Work out' question tests it directly, and the mark scheme rewards evaluating the bracket and the index before the multiplication, penalising a left-to-right reading of the expression."},{"code":"N4","h":"Primes, factors, multiples and prime factorisation","t":"Use the vocabulary of prime numbers, factors (divisors), multiples, common factors and common multiples, and find the highest common factor (HCF) and lowest common multiple (LCM) from the prime factor decomposition of each number, written in index form using product notation (the unique factorisation theorem guarantees this decomposition is the only one for that number). A factor tree is the standard method for finding it. This is basic foundation content; the command word is usually 'Express … as a product of its prime factors' or 'Find the HCF/LCM of …', and the mark scheme gives the method mark for a fully correct tree or list even before the final index form is written."},{"code":"N5","h":"Systematic listing strategies and the product rule for counting","t":"List every possible outcome of a situation systematically — in a consistent order, such as smallest to largest, or one condition fixed while the other varies, using a list, table or diagram — so that none is missed and none is repeated. This systematic listing is basic foundation content, tested by 'list all the ways …' or 'list, in order, the possible outcomes' questions, where the mark scheme checks the list is complete. At Higher tier only, the product rule for counting extends this: multiply the number of choices available at each independent stage to find the total number of outcomes without listing them all, for example the number of three-course meals from a menu with several choices at each course."},{"code":"N6","h":"Integer powers and real roots","t":"Use positive integer powers and their associated roots (square, cube and higher), recognising the powers of 2, 3, 4 and 5, the square numbers up to 15 × 15, and the facts that 1000 = 10³ and 1 million = 10⁶ — this is basic foundation content and must be known instantly, without a calculator, because a 'Write down the value of …' question gives no credit for working. At Higher tier only, estimate the power or root of any given positive number by identifying the two consecutive integers it lies between; the mark scheme awards the method mark for stating both bounding values before giving the estimate."},{"code":"N7","h":"Calculating with roots and indices","t":"Calculate with roots and with integer indices — positive, negative and zero — using the laws of indices, for example that a negative power gives a reciprocal and a power of zero always gives 1. This is additional foundation content, so every student is assessed on it, typically with the command word 'Work out' or 'Simplify' on the non-calculator paper; the mark scheme rewards converting the expression to a single power or root before evaluating it. At Higher tier only, this extends to fractional indices, where the denominator of the fraction gives the root to take and the numerator gives the power then applied to it."},{"code":"N8","h":"Exact calculation: fractions, multiples of π and surds","t":"Give an exact answer rather than a rounded decimal. Basic foundation content requires calculating with fractions and leaving the answer as a fraction in its simplest form; additional foundation content requires leaving an answer as a multiple of π — for example a circle's exact area or circumference — rather than using the calculator's π button and rounding. Both are tested whenever a question says 'give your answer as a fraction' or 'in terms of π', and the mark scheme refuses a rounded decimal even when the value is correct. At Higher tier only, this extends to surds: simplifying one by extracting a square factor (√12 = √4 × √3 = 2√3) and rationalising a denominator by multiplying by a form of 1 that clears the surd from it."},{"code":"N9","h":"Standard form","t":"Calculate with, and interpret, numbers written in standard form, A × 10ⁿ, where 1 ≤ A < 10 and n is an integer, used for very large or very small numbers. This is basic foundation content, examined both with and without a calculator: a non-calculator question might ask you to write a number such as 0.000072 in standard form, while a calculator question requires you to interpret a calculator display such as 4.5E8 as 4.5 × 10⁸. The mark scheme awards the final accuracy mark only when the coefficient A is in the required range, so a correctly calculated but wrongly formatted answer still loses that mark."}],"examples":[{"title":"Subtracting mixed numbers by a formal method","steps":["Write each mixed number as an improper fraction: 3⅔ = 11/3 and 1⅘ = 9/5.","Convert both fractions to a common denominator of 15: 11/3 = 55/15 and 9/5 = 27/15.","Subtract the numerators: 55/15 − 27/15 = 28/15.","Write the answer as a mixed number: 28/15 = 1 13/15."]},{"title":"Priority of operations (BIDMAS): 3 + 4 × 2² − (8 − 5)","steps":["Work out the bracket first: (8 − 5) = 3.","Work out the index: 2² = 4.","Do the multiplication: 4 × 4 = 16.","Add and subtract from left to right: 3 + 16 − 3 = 16."]},{"title":"Prime factor decomposition, HCF and LCM","steps":["Break 360 down with a factor tree: 360 = 2³ × 3² × 5.","Break 84 down the same way: 84 = 2² × 3 × 7.","For the HCF, multiply the lowest power of each prime common to both numbers: 2² × 3 = 12.","For the LCM, multiply the highest power of every prime that appears in either number: 2³ × 3² × 5 × 7 = 2520."]},{"title":"(Higher tier) Evaluating a fractional index","steps":["The denominator of the power tells you which root to take: 27^(1/3) means the cube root of 27.","Work out that root: ∛27 = 3, because 3 × 3 × 3 = 27.","The numerator tells you what power to raise that root to: 27^(2/3) = 3².","Work out the power: 3² = 9."]},{"title":"(Higher tier) Simplifying a surd and rationalising a denominator","steps":["Split 48 into a square factor and the rest: 48 = 16 × 3, so √48 = √16 × √3.","Work out the square root: √16 = 4, so √48 = 4√3.","For 6/√3, multiply the top and bottom by √3 to clear the surd from the denominator: 6/√3 × √3/√3 = 6√3/3.","Simplify the fraction: 6√3/3 = 2√3."]},{"title":"Standard form: converting and calculating","steps":["Count how many places the decimal point moves to sit after the first non-zero digit: 0.000072 → 7.2, a move of 5 places.","Because the original number is less than 1, the power of 10 is negative: 0.000072 = 7.2 × 10⁻⁵.","To multiply (3 × 10⁴) × (2 × 10⁻²), multiply the number parts and add the powers of 10: 3 × 2 = 6 and 10⁴ × 10⁻² = 10².","Write the result in standard form: 6 × 10²."]}],"check":[{"q":"Which is smaller, −4.2 or −4½?","a":"−4½ (= −4.5) is smaller, because it is further from zero on the negative side than −4.2."},{"q":"What is 5 + 3 × (7 − 4)?","a":"14 (work out the bracket, then multiply, then add)."},{"q":"Write 72 as a product of its prime factors.","a":"72 = 2³ × 3²"},{"q":"(Higher tier) Between which two integers does √70 lie?","a":"Between 8 and 9, since 8² = 64 and 9² = 81."},{"q":"Write 3 800 000 in standard form.","a":"3.8 × 10⁶"},{"q":"Given that 43 × 27 = 1161, write down the value of 1161 ÷ 27.","a":"43 (division undoes multiplication, so no calculation is needed)."},{"q":"Write the correct symbol (=, ≠, <, >, ≤ or ≥) between −2¼ and −2.6.","a":"−2¼ > −2.6 (since −2¼ = −2.25, which is greater than −2.6)."}]},"room":{"facts":["The order of operations is brackets, then indices (powers, roots and reciprocals), then division and multiplication, then addition and subtraction.","Multiplication and division are inverse operations, as are squaring and square rooting — spotting them can save you a whole calculation.","Every integer greater than 1 has exactly one prime factor decomposition (the unique factorisation theorem); use it to find the HCF (lowest common powers) and LCM (highest powers) of two numbers.","List outcomes systematically — in a fixed order such as smallest to largest, or with one condition fixed at a time — so that none is missed and none is repeated.","Know the square numbers up to 15 × 15, the cubes of small numbers, the powers of 2, 3, 4 and 5, and that 1000 = 10³ and 1 million = 10⁶.","Laws of indices: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ, a⁰ = 1, a⁻ᵐ = 1/aᵐ.","(Higher tier) Fractional indices: a^(1/n) = ⁿ√a and a^(m/n) = (ⁿ√a)ᵐ.","(Higher tier) To simplify a surd, split the number under the root into a square factor and the rest: √(ab) = √a × √b; to rationalise a denominator, multiply top and bottom by the surd it contains.","Standard form is written A × 10ⁿ, where 1 ≤ A < 10 and n is an integer; n is positive for numbers 10 or greater, zero for numbers from 1 up to just under 10 (e.g. 5 = 5 × 10⁰), and negative for numbers between 0 and 1.","(Higher tier) The product rule for counting: if one stage has m outcomes and an independent second stage has n outcomes, doing both gives m × n possible outcomes altogether.","Give exact answers where the question asks for them: leave a fraction calculation as a fraction in its simplest form, and a circle calculation as a multiple of π, rather than rounding to a decimal."],"questions":[{"q":"Write these numbers in order, starting with the smallest: −2.6, −2¼, 1.8, −3.","a":"−3, −2.6, −2¼, 1.8","sol":"Convert −2¼ to a decimal: −2.25. Compare all four values on a number line: −3 is the furthest left, then −2.6, then −2.25 (−2¼), then 1.8 is the only positive value. So the order is −3, −2.6, −2¼, 1.8.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what it means for one negative number to be smaller than another on a number line.","Convert any fraction to a decimal first, so all four values are in the same form before you compare them.","Start by writing −2¼ as a decimal."],"codes":["N1"]},{"q":"Work out 5 + 3 × (9 − 6)².","a":"32","sol":"Work out the bracket first: 9 − 6 = 3. Square it: 3² = 9. Multiply: 3 × 9 = 27. Add: 5 + 27 = 32.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Remember that an expression like this has a set order to work through — it isn't calculated strictly left to right.","Deal with the bracket and the index before any multiplying, and save the adding until last.","Start by working out what's inside the bracket: 9 − 6."],"codes":["N3"]},{"q":"Write 126 as a product of its prime factors, giving your answer in index form.","a":"126 = 2 × 3² × 7","sol":"Use a factor tree: 126 = 2 × 63 = 2 × 7 × 9 = 2 × 7 × 3 × 3. Grouping the repeated factor in index form: 126 = 2 × 3² × 7.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about which small prime numbers divide exactly into 126.","Keep dividing by a prime number until every branch of your factor tree ends in a prime, then group any repeats as powers.","Start by dividing 126 by 2."],"codes":["N4"]},{"q":"Work out 4³ − √121.","a":"53","sol":"4³ = 4 × 4 × 4 = 64. √121 = 11, since 11 × 11 = 121. 64 − 11 = 53.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["These are results you are expected to know from memory, not calculate from scratch.","Recall the pattern of cube numbers, and the square numbers up to 15 × 15, then combine the two results.","Write 4³ as a repeated multiplication, and think about which number multiplied by itself gives 121."],"codes":["N6"]},{"q":"Write 52 000 in standard form.","a":"5.2 × 10⁴","sol":"Move the decimal point so there is a single non-zero digit before it: 52 000 → 5.2. The point moved 4 places, and the original number is bigger than 1, so the power is positive: 52 000 = 5.2 × 10⁴.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Standard form needs a single non-zero digit before the decimal point, between 1 and 9.","Count how many places the decimal point has to move to get from 52 000 down to that single digit.","Where does the decimal point sit in 52 000 before you move it?"],"codes":["N9"]},{"q":"Work out −2⅗ + 1¾, giving your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"−17/20","sol":"Write each mixed number as an improper fraction: −2⅗ = −13/5 and 1¾ = 7/4. Convert to a common denominator of 20: −13/5 = −52/20 and 7/4 = 35/20. Add: −52/20 + 35/20 = −17/20, which is already in its simplest form.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about how you would add two fractions that do not share a denominator, and what a negative mixed number means.","Turn each mixed number into an improper fraction first, then give them a common denominator.","Start by writing −2⅗ as an improper fraction."],"codes":["N2"]},{"q":"A jacket has a selling price of £68.85 and a cost price of £45.20. The shop sells 24 jackets. Calculate the total profit.","a":"£567.60","sol":"Profit per jacket = selling price − cost price = £68.85 − £45.20 = £23.65. Total profit for 24 jackets = £23.65 × 24 = £567.60.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Profit is the gap between what the shop charges a customer and what it paid to get the item.","Work out the profit on one jacket first, then scale that up to 24 jackets.","Start with £68.85 − £45.20."],"codes":["N2"]},{"q":"Work out 3⁻² × 3⁴.","a":"9","sol":"Use the law of indices for multiplying powers of the same base: add the indices. 3⁻² × 3⁴ = 3^(−2+4) = 3² = 9.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["There's a law of indices for multiplying two powers that share the same base.","Add the two indices together to get a single power of 3, then evaluate it.","Start by writing −2 + 4."],"codes":["N7"]},{"q":"Give the exact area of a circle with radius 5 cm, in terms of π.","a":"25π cm²","sol":"Area of a circle = πr² = π × 5² = π × 25 = 25π cm². Leaving the answer as a multiple of π keeps it exact, rather than rounding a decimal.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about which circle formula gives an area, and what leaving an answer 'exact' means here.","Substitute the radius into the formula and leave π as a symbol instead of using the calculator's π button.","Start with π × 5²."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"List, in a systematic way, all the possible outcomes when a coin is flipped and a fair six-sided dice is rolled at the same time.","a":"H1, H2, H3, H4, H5, H6, T1, T2, T3, T4, T5, T6 (12 outcomes)","sol":"List the coin outcomes first (heads or tails), and for each one list every dice score in order: heads with 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, then tails with 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6. That gives the 12 outcomes: H1, H2, H3, H4, H5, H6, T1, T2, T3, T4, T5, T6.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about how to make sure every combination is listed exactly once, with none missing and none repeated.","Fix the coin's result first and work through every dice score before changing the coin result.","Set out two columns, one for the coin and one for the dice, and work through them systematically starting with the coin showing heads."],"codes":["N5"]},{"q":"Without a calculator, work out (4 × 10³) × (5 × 10⁶), giving your answer in standard form.","a":"2 × 10¹⁰","sol":"Multiply the number parts and add the powers of 10 separately: 4 × 5 = 20 and 10³ × 10⁶ = 10⁹, giving 20 × 10⁹. Because 20 is not between 1 and 10, rewrite it as 2 × 10¹, so the answer is 2 × 10¹ × 10⁹ = 2 × 10¹⁰.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Deal with the number parts and the powers of 10 as two separate calculations.","Multiply the number parts together and add the powers of 10, then check the result is still in standard form.","Start with 4 × 5."],"codes":["N9"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) A café offers a lunch deal: choose 1 of 3 sandwiches, 1 of 4 drinks and 1 of 2 desserts. How many different lunch combinations are possible?","a":"24","sol":"Using the product rule for counting, multiply the number of choices at each independent stage: 3 sandwiches × 4 drinks × 2 desserts = 24 different combinations.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about whether you could work this out by listing every combination, and whether there is a quicker way.","There is a rule for counting combined outcomes across independent, separate stages without listing them all.","Start by writing down the number of choices at each of the three stages: 3, 4 and 2."],"codes":["N5"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Show that √55 lies between 7 and 8, then use trial and improvement to find √55 to 1 decimal place.","a":"7.4","sol":"55 lies between the square numbers 49 (7²) and 64 (8²), so √55 is between 7 and 8. Try 7.4: 7.4² = 54.76, which is just below 55, so √55 is a little more than 7.4. Check the upper boundary for rounding to 1 dp: 7.45² = 55.5025, which is above 55, so √55 is below 7.45. As 7.4 < √55 < 7.45, √55 rounds to 7.4 (1 dp).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about which two square numbers 55 sits between.","Use those two integers as your bounds, then test a decimal between them by squaring it.","Start by comparing 55 with 7² and 8²."],"codes":["N6"]},{"q":"Simplify 2⁵ × 3³ × 7 ÷ (2² × 3³), giving your answer as a product of prime factors in index form.","a":"2³ × 7 (= 56)","sol":"Divide the powers of each prime separately using the law aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ: 2⁵ ÷ 2² = 2³, and 3³ ÷ 3³ = 3⁰ = 1, so the 3s cancel completely. The 7 has no matching prime in the divisor, so it carries through unchanged. The answer is 2³ × 7, which equals 56.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["This still combines writing numbers as products of primes with the rules for dividing powers, but now there are three different primes to track, not two.","Divide the powers of each prime separately using the rule for dividing indices with the same base — and think about what happens to a prime that appears in the first number but not in the second.","Start with 2⁵ ÷ 2²."],"codes":["N4","N7"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) A square has an area of 75 cm². Find the exact side length, giving your answer in the form a√b cm.","a":"5√3 cm","sol":"Side length = √75, since area = side². Split 75 into a square factor and the rest: 75 = 25 × 3, so √75 = √25 × √3 = 5√3 cm.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The side of a square and its area are linked by squaring and square-rooting — think about which one undoes the other.","Look for a square number that divides exactly into 75, so you can pull it out from under the root.","Start by writing the side length as √75."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"A small virus particle has a mass of about 1.5 × 10⁻²⁰ kg. Calculate the total mass, in kg, of 4 × 10²³ virus particles, giving your answer in standard form.","a":"6 × 10³ kg","sol":"Multiply the number parts and add the powers of 10: 1.5 × 4 = 6, and 10⁻²⁰ × 10²³ = 10³, giving a total mass of 6 × 10³ kg.","m":2,"d":3,"hints":["This is a real-world context, but underneath it is a standard-form multiplication.","Multiply the two number parts together and add the two powers of 10 separately.","Start with 1.5 × 4."],"codes":["N9"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Work out 6 × 2 + 3².","a":"21"},{"q":"Write 0.0045 in standard form.","a":"4.5 × 10⁻³"},{"q":"What is the highest common factor of 18 and 30?","a":"6"},{"q":"(Higher tier) Work out 8^(1/3).","a":"2"}]},"cards":[{"code":"N1","front":"Ordering numbers and comparison symbols","back":"On a number line, numbers further left (more negative) are smaller. Symbols: = equal, ≠ not equal, < less than, > greater than, ≤ less than or equal to, ≥ greater than or equal to."},{"code":"N2","front":"Household finance terms","back":"Profit = selling price − cost price; a loss is when cost price is more than selling price. A debit takes money out of an account, a credit puts money in; the balance is what remains."},{"code":"N3","front":"Priority of operations (BIDMAS)","back":"Work out brackets first, then indices (powers, roots, reciprocals), then division and multiplication left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right."},{"code":"N3","front":"Inverse operations","back":"Operations that undo each other: addition undoes subtraction, multiplication undoes division, squaring undoes square rooting. Spotting an inverse pair can save you a whole calculation."},{"code":"N4","front":"Prime factor decomposition","back":"Writing a number as a product of its prime factors in index form, found with a factor tree, e.g. 360 = 2³ × 3² × 5. Every integer greater than 1 has exactly one such decomposition."},{"code":"N4","front":"Finding HCF and LCM from prime factors","back":"HCF = product of the lowest power of each prime common to both numbers. LCM = product of the highest power of every prime that appears in either number."},{"code":"N5","front":"Systematic listing","back":"List every outcome in a fixed order — such as smallest first, or one condition fixed while the other varies — using a list, table or diagram, so none is missed or repeated."},{"code":"N5","front":"(Higher tier) Product rule for counting","back":"If one stage has m outcomes and an independent second stage has n outcomes, doing both gives m × n outcomes in total."},{"code":"N6","front":"Powers and roots to know from memory","back":"The square numbers up to 15 × 15, small cube numbers, the powers of 2, 3, 4 and 5, and the facts 1000 = 10³ and 1 million = 10⁶."},{"code":"N6","front":"(Higher tier) Estimating a root","back":"Find the two consecutive integers whose squares are either side of the number, then test a decimal between them by squaring it."},{"code":"N7","front":"Laws of indices (integer powers)","back":"aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ; (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ; a⁰ = 1; a⁻ᵐ = 1/aᵐ."},{"code":"N7","front":"(Higher tier) Fractional indices","back":"a^(1/n) = ⁿ√a, the nth root of a. a^(m/n) = (ⁿ√a)ᵐ: the denominator gives the root, the numerator gives the power."},{"code":"N8","front":"Exact answers: fractions and multiples of π","back":"Leave a fraction calculation as a fraction in its simplest form, and a circle calculation as a multiple of π (e.g. 25π cm²), instead of rounding to a decimal."},{"code":"N8","front":"(Higher tier) Surds: simplifying and rationalising","back":"Simplify by extracting a square factor: √(ab) = √a × √b, e.g. √12 = 2√3. Rationalise a denominator by multiplying top and bottom by its surd, e.g. 6/√3 = 2√3."},{"code":"N9","front":"Standard form","back":"A × 10ⁿ, where 1 ≤ A < 10 and n is an integer: positive for 10 or more, zero from 1 up to under 10 (e.g. 5 = 5 × 10⁰), negative between 0 and 1."}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae and results to know for Number: Structure and calculation","items":["Priority of operations: work out brackets first, then indices (powers, roots and reciprocals), then division and multiplication (left to right), then addition and subtraction (left to right). This order is not in AQA's formulae appendix — you must apply it automatically.","Area of a circle A = πr²; circumference C = πd = 2πr. These are in the appendix's first group — formulae students must know, so the exam does not give them; they must be recalled from memory.","Laws of indices for integer powers: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ; (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ; a⁰ = 1; a⁻ᵐ = 1/aᵐ. Not in the appendix — required from memory; tested on every tier.","(Higher tier) Fractional indices: a^(1/n) = ⁿ√a; a^(m/n) = (ⁿ√a)ᵐ = ⁿ√(aᵐ). Not in the appendix — required from memory at Higher tier.","(Higher tier) Surd rules: √(ab) = √a × √b and √(a/b) = √a ÷ √b; rationalise a denominator by multiplying top and bottom by the surd it contains. Not in the appendix — required from memory at Higher tier.","Standard form convention: a number written A × 10ⁿ must have 1 ≤ A < 10, with n positive for numbers 10 or greater, zero for numbers from 1 up to just under 10 (e.g. 5 = 5 × 10⁰), and negative for numbers between 0 and 1. Not in the appendix — the range for A is not given and must be known.","Key powers and roots: the square numbers up to 15 × 15, the cubes of numbers up to at least 5, the powers of 2, 3, 4 and 5, and the facts 1000 = 10³ and 1 million = 10⁶. Not in the appendix — required from memory.","Prime factor decomposition method: write a number as a product of its prime factors in index form using a factor tree; HCF = product of the lowest power of each common prime, LCM = product of the highest power of every prime present. Not in the appendix — required from memory.","(Higher tier) The product rule for counting: with m outcomes at one independent stage and n at another, the total number of combined outcomes is m × n. Not in the appendix — required from memory at Higher tier.","Exact-answer convention: a fraction calculation is left as a fraction in its simplest form, and a circle calculation is left as a multiple of π, unless the question asks for a decimal or states an accuracy. Not in the appendix — the convention itself is not given, only the phrase 'give your answer in terms of π' in the question."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Household finance vocabulary (N2)","items":["Cost price is what a shop pays for an item; selling price is what it charges a customer. Profit = selling price − cost price; if cost price is higher than selling price, the result is a loss.","A debit takes money out of an account and a credit puts money in; the balance is what remains once every debit and credit has been applied.","Income tax is a percentage of a person's income paid to the government; VAT (Value Added Tax) is a percentage added to the price of most goods and services.","An interest rate is a percentage used to work out how much interest is added to money saved, or charged on money borrowed, over a given period of time."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-10T20:07:18.867Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Every one of the 16 answer keys, plus all six worked examples, the five checks and the four exit questions, re-solved correctly and independently, and the tier tags are right throughout (Higher only on the product rule, estimating roots, fractional indices and surds, and absent from the additional-foundation π and integer-index questions); the only edits needed before publishing are two difficulty labels, the ambiguous wording of the √55 estimate, the missing n = 0 case on the standard form card, and two small coverage gaps (inverse operations and the comparison symbols)."}}},"3.1.2":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"3.1.2","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Fractions, decimals and percentages are three ways of writing the same value, and AQA expects every student to convert between them fluently, without a calculator on Paper 1 as well as with one on Papers 2 and 3. This room sits inside Number, worth 25% of the Foundation tier and 15% of Higher, but its skills resurface constantly in ratio, proportion and rates-of-change questions (a further 25% Foundation / 20% Higher), so marks lost here cost a student twice over. Converting a decimal to a fraction, reading a fraction inside a ratio, and using a fraction or a percentage as a multiplier are all AO1 recall-and-apply skills — the kind examiners test in short, high-frequency questions across every paper, and the kind that unlocks longer problems in later topics.","idea":[{"h":"Decimals and their corresponding fractions","t":"AQA expects every student, Foundation and Higher, to move fluently between terminating decimals and their corresponding fractions: write the digits after the decimal point over the matching power of ten (tenths, hundredths, thousandths) and simplify, or divide the numerator by the denominator to go the other way. On Higher tier only, this extends to recurring decimals: let x equal the decimal, multiply by a power of 10 to shift one repeating block past the point, subtract the original x, then solve and simplify — and the reverse, writing a fraction as a recurring decimal using AQA's dot notation. Exam wording is typically 'write … as a fraction in its simplest form' or 'write … as a recurring decimal'; the method mark is awarded for the correct place-value fraction (or for setting up the subtraction that removes the recurring part), and the accuracy mark for the simplified result. The notes also flag ordering: mixed lists of decimals and fractions are compared by converting everything to decimals first.","code":"N10"},{"h":"Fractions in ratio problems","t":"This is basic foundation content, so every Foundation and Higher student is expected to handle it confidently. A ratio a : b splits a total into a + b equal parts; one share is a/(a + b) of the whole and the other is b/(a + b), and comparing one share directly with the other, rather than with the whole, gives the fraction a/b. AQA questions ask 'what fraction of the total…' alongside a given ratio, or ask you to 'express as a fraction' a relationship already given as a ratio; some reverse the process, giving a fraction and expecting the ratio to be rebuilt from the numerator and the gap to the denominator. The method mark is for identifying the correct total number of parts, or the correct pair of shares to compare; the accuracy mark is for the fraction fully simplified. This links directly to R8 (ratio, fractions and linear functions) and can appear on any paper, with or without a calculator.","code":"N11"},{"h":"Fractions and percentages as operators","t":"This is basic foundation content: using a fraction or a percentage as an operator means applying it to a quantity to find part of that quantity, not treating it as a value on its own. A fraction of an amount is found by dividing by the denominator and multiplying by the numerator; to find a percentage of an amount, divide the percentage by 100 to get a decimal multiplier, then multiply the amount by it. A percentage increase of r% has multiplier (1 + r/100) and a decrease of r% has multiplier (1 − r/100), giving the new amount directly in one multiplication. Exam wording is 'find', 'work out' or 'calculate' a fraction or percentage of a quantity, and AQA increasingly expects the single-multiplier method rather than find-then-add-or-subtract; the method mark is for the correct multiplier or fraction calculation, the accuracy mark for the final value. This underpins R9 (percentage change) and appears on both calculator and non-calculator papers.","code":"N12"}],"examples":[{"title":"Write 0.375 as a fraction in its simplest form","steps":["0.375 = 375/1000, using three decimal places as thousandths.","375 and 1000 share a factor of 125: divide both by 125.","375 ÷ 125 = 3 and 1000 ÷ 125 = 8, so 0.375 = 3/8."]},{"title":"Order 0.6, 5/8 and 0.65 by size","steps":["Convert 5/8 to a decimal by dividing: 5 ÷ 8 = 0.625.","Compare 0.6, 0.625 and 0.65 digit by digit after the decimal point.","0.6 < 0.625 < 0.65, so in order: 0.6, 5/8, 0.65."]},{"title":"(Higher tier) Write 0.4̇5̇ as a fraction","steps":["Let x = 0.454545… (the digits 45 repeat forever).","Multiply by 100 to shift one full repeating block: 100x = 45.454545…","Subtract the original: 100x − x gives 99x = 45.","Solve and simplify: x = 45/99 = 5/11."]},{"title":"Fractions in a ratio: boys to girls 3 : 5","steps":["Add the parts of the ratio: 3 + 5 = 8 parts in total.","The girls are 5 of those 8 parts, so girls are 5/8 of the class.","Compare girls with boys directly: 5 ÷ 3, so the girls' number is 5/3 of the boys' number."]},{"title":"Find 35% of £240 using a multiplier","steps":["Write 35% as a decimal multiplier: 35 ÷ 100 = 0.35.","Multiply the amount by the multiplier: 240 × 0.35.","240 × 0.35 = 84, so 35% of £240 is £84."]},{"title":"Find the multiplier for a 12% decrease","steps":["A decrease of 12% means the new amount is 100% − 12% of the original.","100% − 12% = 88%, so the multiplier is 88 ÷ 100.","88 ÷ 100 = 0.88, so the multiplier for a 12% decrease is 0.88."]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 0.25 as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"1/4"},{"q":"Convert 3/5 to a decimal.","a":"0.6"},{"q":"In a ratio of 2 : 7, what fraction of the total is the smaller share?","a":"2/9"},{"q":"Find 20% of 150 using a multiplier.","a":"30"}]},"room":{"facts":["To convert a terminating decimal to a fraction, write the digits after the point over the matching power of 10 (tenths, hundredths, thousandths), then simplify.","To convert a fraction to a decimal, divide the numerator by the denominator.","(Higher tier) To convert a recurring decimal to a fraction, let x equal the decimal, multiply by 10ⁿ to shift one repeating block, subtract to cancel the repeating part, then solve for x and simplify.","In a ratio a : b, one share is a/(a+b) of the whole and the other share is b/(a+b) of the whole.","To find a fraction of an amount, divide by the denominator then multiply by the numerator.","To find a percentage of an amount, divide the percentage by 100 to get a decimal multiplier, then multiply.","A percentage increase of r% has multiplier (1 + r/100); a percentage decrease of r% has multiplier (1 − r/100)."],"questions":[{"q":"Write 0.6 as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"3/5","sol":"0.6 = 6/10 (six tenths). Divide numerator and denominator by 2: 6/10 = 3/5, which cannot be simplified further.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Every position after the decimal point stands for a fraction with a power of 10 on the bottom — tenths, hundredths and so on.","Write the decimal as a fraction with that power of 10 as the denominator, then cancel any common factor top and bottom.","0.6 = 6/10"],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"Convert 9/20 to a decimal.","a":"0.45","sol":"Divide the numerator by the denominator: 9 ÷ 20 = 0.45.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A fraction line means exactly the same thing as a division sign.","Divide the numerator by the denominator, carrying on until the division ends.","9 ÷ 20"],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"A garden has flowers to shrubs in the ratio 4 : 3. What fraction of the plants are shrubs?","a":"3/7","sol":"The ratio has 4 + 3 = 7 parts in total. Shrubs are 3 of those 7 parts, so shrubs are 3/7 of the plants.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A ratio splits a total into a number of equal-sized parts.","Add the numbers in the ratio to find the total number of parts, then compare the share you want with that total.","4 + 3 = 7"],"codes":["N11"]},{"q":"Find 1/5 of 60.","a":"12","sol":"Divide 60 by the denominator: 60 ÷ 5 = 12. Only one fifth is needed, so 12 is the answer.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Finding a unit fraction of an amount means splitting it into that many equal pieces.","Divide the amount by the fraction's denominator.","60 ÷ 5"],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"Find 20% of 90 using a multiplier.","a":"18","sol":"20% as a decimal multiplier is 20 ÷ 100 = 0.2. Multiply: 90 × 0.2 = 18.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A percentage can be turned into a single decimal multiplier instead of being applied in two separate steps.","Divide the percentage by 100 to get the multiplier, then multiply the amount by it.","20 ÷ 100 = 0.2"],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"Order these from smallest to largest: 0.7, 3/4, 0.72.","a":"0.7, 0.72, 3/4","sol":"Convert 3/4 to a decimal: 3 ÷ 4 = 0.75. Compare 0.70, 0.72 and 0.75 digit by digit after the point: 0.70 is smallest, then 0.72, then 0.75. So in order: 0.7, 0.72, 3/4.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Values in different forms can't be compared directly until they're written the same way.","Convert every value to a decimal, then line the digits up by place value to compare them.","3 ÷ 4 = 0.75"],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Write 0.2̇7̇ (0.272727…) as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"3/11","sol":"Let x = 0.272727… (the block '27' repeats). Multiply by 100 to shift one full block: 100x = 27.272727… Subtract the original: 100x − x gives 99x = 27. Solve: x = 27/99 = 3/11 after dividing top and bottom by 9.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A repeating decimal can be turned into a fraction by lining up two copies of it and subtracting.","Let x equal the decimal, multiply by the power of 10 that shifts the point past exactly one repeating block, then subtract the original x.","100x = 27.272727…"],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"The ratio of adults to children on a coach is 5 : 9. What fraction of the people on the coach are adults, and what fraction of the children's number is the adults' number?","a":"5/14 of the coach are adults; adults are 5/9 of the children's number","sol":"Add the parts: 5 + 9 = 14 parts in total, so adults are 5/14 of everyone on the coach. To compare adults with children directly, divide adults' parts by children's parts: 5/9 — the adults' number is 5/9 of the children's number.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This question compares two different things, so be clear which two quantities each answer is comparing.","For a share of the whole, compare one part with the total number of parts; for a share of another part, compare the two parts directly.","5 + 9 = 14"],"codes":["N11"]},{"q":"A coat originally costs £85. In a sale it is reduced by 15%. Find the sale price using a multiplier.","a":"£72.25","sol":"A 15% decrease has multiplier 1 − 0.15 = 0.85. Multiply the original price by the multiplier: 85 × 0.85 = £72.25.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A percentage decrease can be applied in a single multiplication rather than working out the discount separately.","Find the multiplier for the decrease, then multiply the original price by it directly.","1 − 0.15 = 0.85"],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"A salary of £24000 is increased by 8%. Find the multiplier for this increase, and use it to find the new salary.","a":"Multiplier 1.08; new salary £25920","sol":"An 8% increase has multiplier 1 + 0.08 = 1.08. Multiply the original salary by the multiplier: 24000 × 1.08 = £25920.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["An increase means the new amount is more than 100% of the original.","Add the percentage increase onto 100% to find the multiplier, then multiply the original amount by it.","100% + 8% = 108%"],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"Three branches price the same item differently: Branch A charges £24. Branch B charges 0.4 of £65. Branch C charges 3/8 of £68. Work out each price and put the branches in order from cheapest to most expensive.","a":"A (£24), C (£25.50), B (£26) — cheapest to most expensive","sol":"Branch A is already £24. Branch B: 0.4 × 65 = £26. Branch C: 68 ÷ 8 × 3 = 8.5 × 3 = £25.50. Comparing £24, £26 and £25.50 gives the order A, then C, then B.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Before three different prices can be compared, each one needs to end up as a single amount of money.","Treat 'of' as multiply: work out what each operator gives when applied to its amount, then compare the three results.","0.4 × 65"],"codes":["N10","N12"]},{"q":"A cake mixture combines flour, sugar and butter in the ratio 9 : 4 : 3. Write sugar's share of the mixture as a fraction in its simplest form, then convert it to a decimal. Which single ingredient makes up more than half the mixture?","a":"Sugar = 1/4 = 0.25; flour makes up more than half (9/16 = 0.5625)","sol":"Total parts: 9 + 4 + 3 = 16. Sugar is 4 of those 16 parts: 4/16, which simplifies to 1/4. As a decimal, 1/4 = 0.25. Flour is 9/16 of the mixture: 9 ÷ 16 = 0.5625, which is more than 0.5, so flour is the ingredient that makes up more than half.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A ratio with three parts still works the same way as one with two — the whole is still split into equal-sized parts.","Add all three numbers in the ratio to find the total parts, then write each ingredient's share as a fraction of that total before converting to decimals.","9 + 4 + 3 = 16"],"codes":["N11","N10"]},{"q":"A charity's donations come from adults and students in the ratio 3 : 5. The charity raises £960 in total. Find 35% of the amount raised by students.","a":"£210","sol":"Total parts: 3 + 5 = 8, and £960 ÷ 8 = £120 per part. Students raised 5 parts: 5 × £120 = £600. 35% of £600 as a multiplier: 600 × 0.35 = £210.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The percentage in this question applies to only one part of the ratio, not the whole amount.","Use the ratio to work out how much the students raised, then apply the percentage multiplier to that amount only.","£960 ÷ 8"],"codes":["N11","N12"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Find 1/3 of 0.7. Give your answer first as a fraction in its simplest form, then as a recurring decimal using dot notation.","a":"7/30; 0.23̇ (0.2333…)","sol":"Write 0.7 as a fraction: 0.7 = 7/10. Multiply by 1/3: (1/3) × (7/10) = 7/30, which is already in its simplest form. Dividing 7 by 30 gives 0.2333…, so as a recurring decimal this is 0.23̇ (the 3 repeats).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Multiplying two fractions is easier once every value is in the same form.","Convert the decimal to a fraction, multiply by the given fraction and simplify, then divide to see which digits repeat.","0.7 = 7/10"],"codes":["N10","N12"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 0.8 as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"4/5"},{"q":"Convert 1/4 to a decimal.","a":"0.25"},{"q":"In a ratio of 2 : 5, what fraction of the total is the larger share?","a":"5/7"},{"q":"Find 10% of 250 using a multiplier.","a":"25"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Terminating decimal → fraction","back":"Write the digits after the decimal point over the matching power of 10 (10, 100, 1000, …), then simplify. E.g. 0.375 = 375/1000 = 3/8.","code":"N10"},{"front":"Fraction → decimal","back":"Divide the numerator by the denominator. E.g. 7/8 = 7 ÷ 8 = 0.875.","code":"N10"},{"front":"(Higher tier) Recurring decimal → fraction","back":"Let x equal the decimal. Multiply by 10ⁿ to shift one repeating block past the point, subtract the original x, then solve and simplify.","code":"N10"},{"front":"(Higher tier) Dot notation","back":"A dot over a single digit shows that digit repeats forever; dots over the first and last digits of a block show the whole block repeats, e.g. 0.4̇5̇ = 0.454545…","code":"N10"},{"front":"Ordering decimals and fractions","back":"Convert every value to a decimal first, then compare digit by digit from the tenths column onwards.","code":"N10"},{"front":"Fraction of a ratio's whole","back":"For a ratio a : b, one share is a/(a+b) of the total and the other share is b/(a+b).","code":"N11"},{"front":"Comparing two shares in a ratio","back":"To compare one part of a ratio directly with another part, not the whole, divide one share by the other — for a : b, the first share is a/b of the second.","code":"N11"},{"front":"Total parts in a ratio","back":"Add every number in the ratio together to find how many equal parts the whole is split into.","code":"N11"},{"front":"Fraction of an amount","back":"Divide the amount by the denominator, then multiply by the numerator.","code":"N12"},{"front":"Percentage of an amount as a multiplier","back":"Divide the percentage by 100 to get a decimal multiplier, then multiply by the amount, e.g. 35% of £240 = 0.35 × 240.","code":"N12"},{"front":"Percentage increase multiplier","back":"For an increase of r%, multiply by (1 + r/100). E.g. an 8% increase multiplies by 1.08.","code":"N12"},{"front":"Percentage decrease multiplier","back":"For a decrease of r%, multiply by (1 − r/100). E.g. a 15% decrease multiplies by 0.85.","code":"N12"},{"front":"Operator, not a value","back":"A fraction or percentage used as an operator acts on a quantity to find part of it; apply it by multiplying, not by adding or subtracting on its own.","code":"N12"}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Fractions, decimals and percentages — results to know from memory","items":["Terminating decimal → fraction: write the digits after the decimal point over the place-value denominator (10, 100, 1000, …), then simplify — e.g. 0.375 = 375/1000 = 3/8. Not in AQA's formulae Appendix — you must know this method from memory.","Fraction → decimal: divide the numerator by the denominator — e.g. 7/8 = 7 ÷ 8 = 0.875. Not given in the exam.","(Higher tier) Recurring decimal → fraction: let x = the decimal, multiply by 10ⁿ (n = length of the repeating block) to line up the repeating digits, subtract the original x, then solve and simplify — e.g. x = 0.4̇5̇ gives 99x = 45, so x = 5/11. Not given in the exam; this method is not in the Appendix.","(Higher tier) Fraction → recurring decimal: divide the numerator by the denominator and mark the repeating digit(s) with a dot (one dot for a single repeating digit, dots over the first and last digits of a longer block) — e.g. 7/30 = 0.2333… = 0.23̇. Not given in the exam.","Fraction of an amount = amount × numerator ÷ denominator (divide by the denominator, then multiply by the numerator). Not given in the exam.","Percentage of an amount = amount × (percentage ÷ 100), i.e. amount × the decimal multiplier. Not given in the exam.","Percentage change multiplier: an increase of r% is ×(1 + r/100); a decrease of r% is ×(1 − r/100). AQA's Appendix lists compound interest among the formulae students must know or be able to derive — it is not printed in the paper either.","Ratio a : b as fractions of the whole: the two shares are a/(a+b) and b/(a+b) of the total. Not given in the exam.","Ratio a : b compared part-to-part: the first share is a/b of the second share, not of the whole. Not given in the exam."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Common fraction–decimal–percentage equivalences worth knowing instantly","items":["1/2 = 0.5 = 50%","1/4 = 0.25 = 25%; 3/4 = 0.75 = 75%","1/5 = 0.2 = 20%; 2/5 = 0.4 = 40%; 3/5 = 0.6 = 60%; 4/5 = 0.8 = 80%","1/8 = 0.125 = 12.5%; 3/8 = 0.375 = 37.5%","1/10 = 0.1 = 10%","1/3 = 0.333… ≈ 33.3%","(Higher tier) 1/3 = 0.3̇ exactly, using dot notation for the digit that repeats forever","Knowing these instantly speeds up ordering questions: convert everything to decimals, then compare digit by digit."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-10T19:52:19.638Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"All fourteen answer keys and both the check and exit sets re-solve correctly and every solution matches its answer, and the Higher-tier tagging on recurring decimals is right throughout — publish after fixing the compound-interest claim in the formulae sheet, the garbled percentage sentence in idea[2], and the hint at question 9 that hands over the multiplier the question asks for."}}},"3.1.3":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"3.1.3","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Every AQA paper mixes exact algebra with real measurements, so marks are lost whenever a student converts units the wrong way, trusts an unreasonable calculator answer, rounds too early, or states a bound the wrong way round. This room builds the metric conversion factors and compound-measure formulae you must recall, plus the exact inequality conventions AQA's mark schemes demand for error intervals and — at Higher tier — bounds, so accuracy marks are never lost to a habit rather than a genuine misunderstanding. These skills also sit underneath Geometry and measures (15% Foundation / 20% Higher) and any calculation question set in a real-life context, so this room is tested far more often than its own small section suggests.","idea":[{"h":"Standard units and compound measures","t":"N13 is basic foundation content, so every student is expected to convert confidently between metric units of length, area, volume and capacity, and to work with standard compound measures such as speed, density and pressure. You must know the metric conversion factors from memory — they are never given in the exam — and remember to square the factor when converting an area and cube it when converting a volume. Imperial-to-metric conversions, such as miles to kilometres, are always given in the question, so you only need to apply them, not recall them. Questions are usually worded 'Convert … to …', or use the command word 'Calculate', and often embed the conversion inside a real-life context such as a recipe, a journey or a container; the method mark is for multiplying or dividing by the correct conversion factor (using its reciprocal is a common lost mark), and the accuracy mark is for the correctly converted value with the right units.","code":"N13"},{"h":"Estimation and checking","t":"N14 is basic foundation content that can be assessed on any paper, including the calculator papers, because it tests whether a student can judge whether an answer is sensible. The technique is to round every number in the calculation to 1 significant figure (unless told otherwise), work out the simplified calculation, and compare the result with any calculator answer given. The command word 'Estimate' expects the rounded values to be shown as the method — the method mark is for correctly rounding every number to 1 s.f., and the accuracy mark is for evaluating the rounded expression correctly. AQA also expects students to evaluate results obtained using technology: if a calculator answer is wildly different from the estimate, say so and explain what might have gone wrong, such as a misplaced decimal point.","code":"N14"},{"h":"Rounding and error intervals","t":"Rounding to a given number of decimal places or significant figures is basic foundation content; writing an error interval is additional foundation, so both are examined on the Foundation tier as well as Higher. For rounding, look at the digit immediately after the place being kept. For an error interval, use the value's rounding or truncation accuracy to find how far the true value could lie above or below it, and write it using AQA's convention: lower bound ≤ true value < upper bound, since the upper bound itself would round up to the next value. The command phrase is usually 'write down the error interval for…'; the marks are for the two correct values and for writing them with the correct inequality signs. Never round values in the middle of a calculation — only round the final answer.","code":"N15"},{"h":"Limits of accuracy and bounds","t":"Applying and interpreting limits of accuracy is additional foundation content: given the error interval of a measurement, use the smallest or largest value it could be to answer a practical question, such as whether a claim about that measurement is definitely true. (Higher tier) Calculating the upper or lower bound of a quantity worked out from more than one rounded measurement — such as an area, a perimeter or a compound measure — is Higher only. The rule is: for the largest sum or product, use the upper bound of every part, and for the smallest, use the lower bound of every part; for a subtraction or a division, mix them — UB ÷ LB (or UB − LB) for the largest, LB ÷ UB (or LB − UB) for the smallest. Exam questions use phrases like 'work out the upper bound' or 'give your answer to a suitable degree of accuracy', and the final method mark is for rounding the calculated bound sensibly — only as precisely as the upper and lower bounds still agree.","code":"N16"}],"examples":[{"title":"Converting units of area and capacity","steps":["1 m = 100 cm, so 1 m² = 100 × 100 = 10 000 cm².","2.5 m² = 2.5 × 10 000 = 25 000 cm².","1 litre = 1000 cm³.","0.75 litres = 0.75 × 1000 = 750 cm³."]},{"title":"Estimating a calculation by rounding to 1 significant figure","steps":["Round each number to 1 significant figure: 4.86 ≈ 5, 19.7 ≈ 20, 0.51 ≈ 0.5.","Estimate = (5 × 20) ÷ 0.5.","5 × 20 = 100, then 100 ÷ 0.5 = 200.","4.86 and 19.7 were both rounded up and 0.51 was rounded down, and both changes increase a division's result, so the estimate of 200 is bigger than the exact answer."]},{"title":"Rounding to a given number of significant figures","steps":["The first significant figure is the 3 (the first non-zero digit), so the three significant figures are 3, 0 and 4.","The next digit after these three is 6, which rounds the third figure up: 4 becomes 5.","0.030461 rounds to 0.0305 (3 s.f.), keeping the leading zeros that fix the place value."]},{"title":"Writing an error interval","steps":["8.3 cm is rounded to 1 decimal place, so the rounding unit is 0.1 cm and half of that is 0.05 cm.","Lower bound = 8.3 − 0.05 = 8.25 cm.","Upper bound = 8.3 + 0.05 = 8.35 cm, but 8.35 would itself round up to 8.4, so it must be excluded.","Error interval: 8.25 ≤ l < 8.35."]},{"title":"Applying a limit of accuracy: Jerry needs a plank at least 2.35 m long. A plank is labelled 2.4 m, to the nearest 10 cm. Can he be sure it is long enough?","steps":["2.4 m is rounded to the nearest 10 cm (0.1 m), so half the rounding unit is 0.05 m.","2.4 − 0.05 = 2.35 m.","The shortest the plank can be is exactly 2.35 m, so yes, it is long enough."]},{"title":"(Higher tier) The upper bound of a calculated area","steps":["6.4 cm is rounded to 2 s.f., so its bounds are 6.35 cm to 6.45 cm.","3.2 cm is rounded to 2 s.f., so its bounds are 3.15 cm to 3.25 cm.","Upper bound of area = 6.45 × 3.25 = 20.9625 cm².","Lower bound of area = 6.35 × 3.15 = 20.0025 cm².","20.0025 rounds to 20 and 20.9625 rounds to 21 at the nearest whole number, so only 1 significant figure is safe: the area is 20 cm² to a suitable degree of accuracy."]}],"check":[{"q":"What is 1 m² in cm²?","a":"10 000 cm²."},{"q":"Why might you round each number to 1 significant figure before estimating a calculation?","a":"It makes the numbers easy to calculate mentally, so you can quickly check whether an exact or calculator answer is sensible."},{"q":"A mass is given as 6.0 kg to 1 decimal place. Write its error interval.","a":"5.95 ≤ m < 6.05 (kg)."},{"q":"A pole is 3.5 m long, to the nearest 0.1 m. What is the shortest possible length of the pole?","a":"3.45 m."}]},"room":{"facts":["10 mm = 1 cm, 100 cm = 1 m, 1000 m = 1 km.","1 m² = 10 000 cm²; 1 cm² = 100 mm² (square the length factor to convert an area).","1000 cm³ = 1 litre; 1 cm³ = 1 ml (cube the length factor to convert a volume).","1000 mg = 1 g, 1000 g = 1 kg, 1000 kg = 1 tonne.","60 s = 1 min, 60 min = 1 h.","Speed = distance ÷ time; density = mass ÷ volume; pressure = force ÷ area.","Rounded to accuracy a: (value − ½a) ≤ x < (value + ½a).","Truncated to accuracy a: value ≤ x < value + a.","(Higher tier) To find the upper bound of a ÷ b, divide the upper bound of a by the lower bound of b."],"questions":[{"q":"Write 2 hours 15 minutes in minutes.","a":"135 minutes.","sol":"1 hour = 60 minutes, so 2 hours = 2 × 60 = 120 minutes. 120 + 15 = 135 minutes.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Recall how many minutes make one hour.","Convert the hours to minutes first, then add on the extra minutes.","2 hours = 2 × 60 minutes, so start with 120 + 15."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"Write 2.4 kg in grams.","a":"2400 g.","sol":"1 kg = 1000 g, so 2.4 kg = 2.4 × 1000 = 2400 g.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Recall the link between kilograms and grams.","Multiply the number of kilograms by the conversion factor to grams.","1 kg = 1000 g, so start with 2.4 × 1000."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"A tabletop has an area of 1.8 m². What is this area in cm²?","a":"18 000 cm².","sol":"1 m = 100 cm, so 1 m² = 100 × 100 = 10 000 cm². 1.8 m² = 1.8 × 10 000 = 18 000 cm².","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what happens to a conversion factor when you're converting an area rather than a length.","Square the linear conversion factor between metres and centimetres before multiplying.","1 m² = 100² cm² = 10 000 cm², so start with 1.8 × 10 000."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"(calculator) A car uses 6.4 litres of fuel per 100 km. Petrol costs £1.48 per litre. Find the cost of the fuel for a 350 km journey.","a":"£33.15.","sol":"Fuel used = (350 ÷ 100) × 6.4 = 3.5 × 6.4 = 22.4 litres. Cost = 22.4 × £1.48 = £33.152, which rounds to £33.15 to the nearest penny.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Break the problem into two compound-measure steps: how much fuel is used, then what that fuel costs.","Scale the fuel rate to the actual distance travelled before multiplying by the price per litre.","Start with fuel used = (350 ÷ 100) × 6.4."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"Estimate the value of 39 × 5.2 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"≈200.","sol":"39 ≈ 40 and 5.2 ≈ 5 (both to 1 s.f.). Estimate = 40 × 5 = 200.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Round each number to a value that's easy to work with in your head.","Round both 39 and 5.2 to one significant figure before multiplying.","39 ≈ 40 and 5.2 ≈ 5, so start with 40 × 5."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"Estimate the value of (212 × 0.48) ÷ 5.3 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"≈20.","sol":"212 ≈ 200, 0.48 ≈ 0.5, 5.3 ≈ 5 (all to 1 s.f.). Estimate = (200 × 0.5) ÷ 5 = 100 ÷ 5 = 20.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Simplify every number in the calculation before you combine them.","Round each of the three numbers to 1 significant figure, then work out the rounded calculation.","212 ≈ 200, 0.48 ≈ 0.5, 5.3 ≈ 5, so start with (200 × 0.5) ÷ 5."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"A shop assistant uses a calculator to work out 19% of £406 and gets £771.40. Use estimation to explain why this answer must be wrong, and work out the correct amount.","a":"£77.14 — 20% of £400 ≈ £80, so £771.40 is ten times too big; the decimal point has been misplaced.","sol":"Estimate: 19% ≈ 20% and £406 ≈ £400, so the answer should be about 20% of £400 = £80. The calculator answer of £771.40 is nowhere near this estimate, so it must be wrong. Correct: 19% of £406 = 0.19 × 406 = £77.14, which matches the £80 estimate — the calculator's £771.40 is exactly 10 times this, so the decimal point has been misplaced by one place.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Think about roughly what size the answer should be before trusting the calculator display.","Round 19% to 20% and £406 to £400, then estimate 20% of that rounded amount.","Start with 20% of £400, i.e. 0.2 × 400."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"Round 0.030 461 to 3 significant figures.","a":"0.0305.","sol":"The first significant figure is the 3 (hundredths place), so the three significant figures are 3, 0 and 4. The next digit is 6, which rounds the third figure up: 4 becomes 5. So 0.030461 rounds to 0.0305 (3 s.f.).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Find the first non-zero digit — significant figures are counted from there.","Identify the three significant figures being kept, then look at the next digit to decide whether to round up.","The first three significant figures are 3, 0 and 4; the next digit is 6."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"Round 5.6784 to 2 decimal places.","a":"5.68.","sol":"The digit after the second decimal place is 8, which rounds the second decimal place up: 5.6784 rounds to 5.68 (2 d.p.).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Look at the digit just after the place you're rounding to.","Decide whether that next digit rounds the second decimal place up or leaves it the same.","5.6784: the digit after the second decimal place is 8."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"A distance is measured as 240 m, to the nearest 10 m. Write the error interval for the true distance, d.","a":"235 ≤ d < 245 (m).","sol":"The rounding unit is 10 m, so half a unit is 5 m. Lower bound = 240 − 5 = 235 m. Upper bound = 240 + 5 = 245 m, and 245 is excluded because it would itself round up to 250. Error interval: 235 ≤ d < 245.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about how far the true distance could be from 240 m and still round to 240 m.","Halve the rounding unit to find how much you can add to or subtract from 240.","Half of 10 m is 5 m, so start with the lower bound 240 − 5."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"(calculator) Jess calculates √20 ÷ 3 by first rounding √20 to 4.5, then dividing by 3 to get 1.5. Explain why Jess's method could give an inaccurate answer, and find the correct answer to 2 decimal places.","a":"1.49 (2 d.p.) — rounding √20 to 4.5 too early gives the less accurate 1.50; the correct value is 1.49.","sol":"Rounding √20 to 4.5 before dividing loses accuracy, because that rounding error carries through the rest of the calculation. Using the unrounded value: √20 = 4.472135…, so 4.472135… ÷ 3 = 1.490711…, which rounds to 1.49 (2 d.p.) — not the 1.50 Jess found by rounding too early.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what happens to a rounding error once it's carried into a further calculation.","Work out the division using the full, unrounded value of √20 rather than a rounded version.","√20 = 4.472135… (calculator value), so start with 4.472135… ÷ 3."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"A number is truncated to 2 decimal places to give 3.14. Write the error interval for the exact value, t.","a":"3.14 ≤ t < 3.15.","sol":"Truncating cuts off the digits after 2 decimal places rather than rounding, so the exact value must be at least 3.14 but can be anything up to (but not reaching) the next hundredth. Error interval: 3.14 ≤ t < 3.15.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Truncating is different from rounding — think about whether digits are simply cut off or rounded up or down.","Decide the smallest and largest values that would all truncate to give 3.14.","The exact value can't be smaller than the truncated figure itself — think about how much bigger it could be before it would truncate to the next hundredth."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"A recipe needs 250 g of flour, correct to the nearest 10 g. Priya has exactly 245 g of flour. Can she be sure she has enough?","a":"No — the recipe could need up to (just under) 255 g.","sol":"The stated 250 g is rounded to the nearest 10 g, so the true amount needed satisfies 245 ≤ amount < 255 (g). Priya's 245 g only covers the lowest possible requirement; if the true amount needed is anything more than 245 g, which is possible, she will not have enough. So she cannot be sure.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the full range of amounts that would all round to 250 g.","Work out the whole range of amounts the recipe could need, then ask whether her 245 g covers the largest of them.","The rounding unit is 10 g, so start with the lower bound 250 − 5."],"codes":["N16"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) (calculator) A runner covers a distance of 100 m, measured to the nearest metre, in a time of 15.2 seconds, measured to the nearest 0.1 seconds. Work out the upper bound of the runner's average speed, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"6.63 m/s (3 s.f.).","sol":"Distance bounds (nearest 1 m): 99.5 ≤ distance < 100.5. Time bounds (nearest 0.1 s): 15.15 ≤ time < 15.25. To maximise speed = distance ÷ time, use the upper bound of distance and the lower bound of time: 100.5 ÷ 15.15 = 6.6336…, which rounds to 6.63 m/s (3 s.f.).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Think about which bound of each measurement makes a quotient as large as possible.","Start by finding the bounds of the distance (nearest 1 m) and the time (nearest 0.1 s).","So start with 100.5 ÷ 15.15."],"codes":["N13","N16"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) A rectangle measures 6.4 cm by 3.2 cm, each measured to 2 significant figures. Find the upper bound of the area, then give the area to a suitable degree of accuracy.","a":"Upper bound 20.9625 cm²; area = 20 cm² (1 s.f.).","sol":"6.4 cm (2 s.f.) has bounds 6.35 cm to 6.45 cm; 3.2 cm (2 s.f.) has bounds 3.15 cm to 3.25 cm. Upper bound of area = 6.45 × 3.25 = 20.9625 cm². Lower bound of area = 6.35 × 3.15 = 20.0025 cm². These round to 21 and 20 respectively at 2 significant figures, so only 1 significant figure is safe: the area is 20 cm² to a suitable degree of accuracy.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Think about which bound of each side makes the area as large as possible.","Start by finding the bounds of each side length (each given to 2 significant figures).","So start with 6.45 × 3.25."],"codes":["N16"]},{"q":"A plank of wood is 1.8 m long, to the nearest 0.1 m. What is the shortest possible length of the plank?","a":"1.75 m.","sol":"The rounding unit is 0.1 m, so half a unit is 0.05 m. The shortest it can be is 1.8 − 0.05 = 1.75 m.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about how far below 1.8 m a length could be and still round to 1.8 m.","Subtract half the rounding unit from 1.8 m.","Half of 0.1 m is 0.05 m, so start with 1.8 − 0.05."],"codes":["N16"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 4.5 litres in cm³.","a":"4500 cm³."},{"q":"Estimate 58 × 2.9 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"≈180 (60 × 3)."},{"q":"A length is 12 cm, to the nearest cm. Write its error interval.","a":"11.5 ≤ l < 12.5 (cm)."},{"q":"A number rounds to 7.0 to 1 decimal place. Write its error interval.","a":"6.95 ≤ x < 7.05."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Metric length conversions","back":"10 mm = 1 cm, 100 cm = 1 m, 1000 m = 1 km.","code":"N13"},{"front":"Metric area conversions","back":"1 cm² = 100 mm²; 1 m² = 10 000 cm². Square the length factor to convert an area.","code":"N13"},{"front":"Metric volume and capacity conversions","back":"1 cm³ = 1 ml; 1000 cm³ = 1 litre; 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³. Cube the length factor to convert a volume.","code":"N13"},{"front":"Standard compound measures","back":"Speed = distance ÷ time; density = mass ÷ volume; pressure = force ÷ area.","code":"N13"},{"front":"Estimating a calculation","back":"Round every number in the calculation to 1 significant figure, then work out the simplified calculation to get an approximate answer.","code":"N14"},{"front":"Checking a calculator answer","back":"Compare the calculator's answer with your estimate. If they're very different, check for a misplaced decimal point or a wrongly pressed key.","code":"N14"},{"front":"Rounding to decimal places or significant figures","back":"Look at the digit after the place you're rounding to: 5 or more rounds the previous digit up, below 5 leaves it the same.","code":"N15"},{"front":"Error interval for a rounded value","back":"For a value rounded to accuracy a, the true value x satisfies (value − ½a) ≤ x < (value + ½a).","code":"N15"},{"front":"Error interval for a truncated value","back":"Truncating just cuts off extra digits. If a value truncates to v at accuracy a, then v ≤ x < (v + a).","code":"N15"},{"front":"Don't round early","back":"Never round a value part-way through a calculation — round only the final answer, or the error carries through the rest of the working.","code":"N15"},{"front":"Why the error interval has ≤ and <","back":"The smallest possible value is included (≤) because rounding it would give exactly the stated value; the largest possible value is excluded (<) because it would round up to the next one.","code":"N15"},{"front":"Applying a limit of accuracy to a claim","back":"To check whether a claim is definitely true, test it against the least favourable value the measurement could take — if it fails there, you cannot be sure.","code":"N16"},{"front":"(Higher tier) Bounds of a calculated quantity","back":"To maximise a product or sum, use the upper bound of every part. To maximise a ÷ b or a − b, use the upper bound of a and the lower bound of b.","code":"N16"}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae and facts to know from memory","items":["10 mm = 1 cm. Not given in the exam — you must know this.","100 cm = 1 m. Not given in the exam — you must know this.","1000 m = 1 km. Not given in the exam — you must know this.","1 cm² = 100 mm² (square the length factor). Not given in the exam — you must know this.","1 m² = 10 000 cm² (square the length factor). Not given in the exam — you must know this.","1 cm³ = 1 ml. Not given in the exam — you must know this.","1000 cm³ = 1 litre. Not given in the exam — you must know this.","1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³ (cube the length factor). Not given in the exam — you must know this.","1000 mg = 1 g. Not given in the exam — you must know this.","1000 g = 1 kg. Not given in the exam — you must know this.","1000 kg = 1 tonne. Not given in the exam — you must know this.","60 s = 1 min. Not given in the exam — you must know this.","60 min = 1 h. Not given in the exam — you must know this.","Imperial/metric conversions (eg 5 miles ≈ 8 km, 1 kg ≈ 2.2 pounds): given in the question — you are not expected to recall them.","Speed = distance ÷ time. Not given in the exam — you must know this.","Density = mass ÷ volume. Not given in the exam — you must know this.","Pressure = force ÷ area. Not given in the exam — you must know this.","Error interval — rounded to accuracy a: v − ½a ≤ x < v + ½a; truncated to accuracy a: v ≤ x < v + a. Not given in the exam — you must know this rule."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"(Higher tier) Combining bounds in a calculation","items":["(Higher tier) To maximise a sum or a product, use the upper bound of every measurement involved.","(Higher tier) To minimise a sum or a product, use the lower bound of every measurement involved.","(Higher tier) To maximise a ÷ b or a − b, use the upper bound of a and the lower bound of b.","(Higher tier) To minimise a ÷ b or a − b, use the lower bound of a and the upper bound of b.","(Higher tier) Only give a final answer to the degree of accuracy at which the upper and lower bounds round to the same value."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-10T21:09:07.782Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"The mathematics is sound end to end — all sixteen answer keys, every error interval, both Higher bound calculations (100.5 ÷ 15.15 = 6.63 m/s and 6.45 × 3.25 = 20.9625 cm²) and all six worked examples recompute exactly, and the Higher-tier tags land on precisely the bounds-of-a-calculated-quantity material and nowhere else — so the only edits needed before publishing are trimming the unasked second half of questions[14].sol and telling the formulae sheet that imperial/metric conversions are given in the question."}}},"3.2.1":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"3.2.1","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Algebraic notation and manipulation is the language every other algebra topic is written in — equations, formulae, sequences, graphs and Higher-tier proof all assume fluent reading and writing of this notation, and confident simplifying, expanding, factorising and rearranging without a calculator. Algebra carries 20% of Foundation marks and 30% of Higher marks — the single largest content area at Higher tier — and every paper mixes these skills into longer, multi-step questions — so marks are lost not for a wrong method but for an unsimplified answer, a sign slip when expanding, or a formula left in the wrong subject, even when the working shown is otherwise correct.","idea":[{"h":"Algebraic notation","t":"A1 sets the alphabet every later algebra question is written in: ab means a × b, 3y means y + y + y or 3 × y, a² means a × a, a²b means a × a × b, and a/b means a ÷ b, with coefficients written as fractions such as x/2 rather than decimals such as 0.5x. This is basic foundation content, so every student is marked on it: examiners expect an expression given in this condensed, simplest form even when the question does not say 'simplify', and a correct value written in the wrong notation — 2.5x instead of 5x/2, say, or a × a left unexpanded — can cost the accuracy mark.","code":"A1"},{"h":"Substitution into formulae and expressions","t":"A2 asks you to replace letters with given numbers in a formula or expression and evaluate the result, including formulae from other subjects, which the question always supplies in full if they are unfamiliar. This basic foundation skill is tested with command words such as 'Work out' or 'Find the value of', and the mark scheme gives a method mark for substituting correctly — each value in the right place, with negative numbers in brackets — before the accuracy mark for the evaluated answer, so a slip in the arithmetic after a correct substitution still banks the method mark.","code":"A2"},{"h":"Algebraic vocabulary","t":"A3 is the vocabulary that lets a question tell you what to do without extra explanation: an expression has no equals sign, an equation is only true for particular values of the unknown, a formula connects two or more variables, and an inequality compares two expressions with <, >, ≤, ≥ or ≠ instead of an equals sign — while, additional foundation content, an identity (≡) is true for every value of the letter, and a term is one part of an expression and a factor divides exactly into it. Examiners test this both implicitly, by using the precise word in a question and expecting the matching response, and explicitly, by asking you to classify a statement or justify your choice.","code":"A3"},{"h":"Simplifying, expanding and factorising","t":"A4 is the manipulation toolkit used in nearly every algebra question. Basic foundation content — collecting like terms, multiplying a single term over a bracket, taking out common factors, and simplifying powers with the laws of indices — is tested with 'Simplify' or 'Factorise'. Additional foundation content — expanding two binomials and factorising quadratics of the form x² + bx + c, including the difference of two squares, and manipulating expressions involving surds — is tested with 'Expand and simplify' or 'Factorise', where the method mark rewards the correctly expanded terms before they are collected. (Higher tier) students also expand three or more binomials and factorise ax² + bx + c, where the mark scheme rewards the correct pair of numbers — multiplying to a × c and summing to b — before the final factorised form, and manipulate algebraic fractions.","code":"A4"},{"h":"Standard formulae and changing the subject","t":"A5 covers using a standard mathematical formula — from maths or another subject, given in words or symbols — and rearranging a formula to change its subject. This is basic foundation content, tested with 'Rearrange to make x the subject' or 'Make r the subject of the formula'. The mark scheme awards a method mark for each correct inverse operation applied to both sides in the right order, and the accuracy mark for isolating the subject on its own on one side, so a rearrangement left as, say, r² = A/π rather than r = √(A/π) misses the final mark.","code":"A5"},{"h":"Equations, identities and algebraic argument","t":"A6, additional foundation content, asks you to tell an equation from an identity and to argue algebraically that two expressions are equivalent, using the identity symbol ≡. This is tested with 'Show that', where every line of working must follow from the one before and the printed result must be reached exactly — a correct answer with steps skipped earns no method marks. (Higher tier) extends this to full proofs, such as showing a result holds for every integer, where the mark scheme rewards a general algebraic argument, using letters such as n, n + 1 or 2n for any, consecutive or even integers, rather than a check on particular numbers.","code":"A6"},{"h":"Functions, inverse and composite functions","t":"A7, basic foundation content, treats an expression as a function machine with an input and an output, described in words rather than symbols. (Higher tier) this extends to formal f(x) notation, the inverse function f⁻¹(x), which reverses the process, and the composite function fg(x), meaning apply g first and then f. Foundation questions describe the operations in words; Higher questions use f(x) notation directly, and the mark scheme gives a method mark for substituting correctly — into g first for a composite function, or for swapping the letters before rearranging to find an inverse — and an accuracy mark for the simplified result.","code":"A7"}],"examples":[{"title":"Write 3 × a × a × b ÷ 2 in algebraic notation","steps":["Write the repeated multiplication as a power: a × a = a².","Multiply that power by the remaining letter and the number: 3 × a² × b = 3a²b.","Rewrite the division by 2 as a fraction: 3 × a × a × b ÷ 2 = 3a²b/2."]},{"title":"Find v when u = 4, a = −2 and t = 3, using v = u + at","steps":["Write the formula with each value in place, putting the negative number in brackets: v = 4 + (−2) × 3.","Work out the multiplication first: (−2) × 3 = −6.","Add the two values: v = 4 + (−6) = −2."]},{"title":"Expand and simplify (x + 3)(x − 5)","steps":["Multiply each term in the first bracket by each term in the second: x × x, x × (−5), 3 × x, 3 × (−5).","Work out each of the four products: x² − 5x + 3x − 15.","Collect the two middle terms: x² − 2x − 15."]},{"title":"Make r the subject of A = πr²","steps":["Divide both sides by π to isolate r²: A/π = r².","Take the square root of both sides: √(A/π) = r.","Write the formula with r as the subject: r = √(A/π)."]},{"title":"Show that (n + 1)² − (n − 1)² is a multiple of 4","steps":["Expand each bracket separately: (n + 1)² = n² + 2n + 1 and (n − 1)² = n² − 2n + 1.","Subtract the second expansion from the first: (n² + 2n + 1) − (n² − 2n + 1) = 4n.","4n is 4 multiplied by an integer, so (n + 1)² − (n − 1)² is always a multiple of 4."]},{"title":"(Higher tier) Find fg(2), given f(x) = 3x + 2 and g(x) = x²","steps":["Work out the inner function first, since fg(x) means apply g, then f: g(2) = 2² = 4.","Substitute that result into f: f(4) = 3 × 4 + 2.","Work out the final value: 12 + 2 = 14, so fg(2) = 14."]}],"check":[{"q":"What does a²b mean in algebraic notation?","a":"a × a × b."},{"q":"If v = u + at, find v when u = 5, a = 2 and t = 3.","a":"v = 11."},{"q":"Is 2(x + 3) = 2x + 6 an equation or an identity? How do you know?","a":"An identity — expanding the bracket gives exactly 2x + 6, so it is true for every value of x, not just one."},{"q":"Expand and simplify (x + 2)(x + 5).","a":"x² + 7x + 10."},{"q":"(Higher tier) Find f⁻¹(x) if f(x) = 2x − 3.","a":"f⁻¹(x) = (x + 3)/2."}]},"room":{"facts":["Laws of indices: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ; (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ; a⁰ = 1.","The difference of two squares: a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b).","An expression has no equals sign; an equation is true only for particular values of the unknown; a formula connects two or more variables; an identity (≡) is true for every value.","To change the subject of a formula, apply the same inverse operation to both sides, undoing the operations on the subject in reverse order.","(Higher tier) f⁻¹(x) reverses what f does to an input; fg(x) means work out g first, then apply f to that result."],"questions":[{"q":"Write 5 × p × p × q using index notation.","a":"5p²q","sol":"Group the repeated letter as a power: p × p = p². Bring in the number and the remaining letter: 5 × p² × q = 5p²q.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about how a letter multiplied by itself is written in algebraic notation.","Group any identical letters together first, then bring in the number and any different letters.","p × p = p²"],"codes":["A1"]},{"q":"Evaluate 3x² − 2x when x = −2.","a":"16","sol":"Substitute x = −2, working out the power first: 3 × (−2)² − 2 × (−2) = 3 × 4 − (−4) = 12 + 4 = 16.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Substituting a negative number into a power needs extra care with brackets.","Put the negative value in brackets everywhere it appears, then work out the power before you multiply or subtract.","3 × (−2)²"],"codes":["A2"]},{"q":"State whether 3a + 7 is an expression, an equation, a formula or an identity, and give a reason.","a":"An expression","sol":"3a + 7 has no equals sign, so it cannot be true or false and cannot be solved for a value of a — that makes it an expression.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what separates an expression from the other three kinds of algebraic statement.","Check whether the statement contains an equals sign, and if it does, what kind of equals sign it is.","3a + 7 — no equals sign anywhere in it"],"codes":["A3"]},{"q":"Factorise 6x + 9 fully.","a":"3(2x + 3)","sol":"3 is the highest common factor of 6 and 9, so take it outside a bracket: 6x + 9 = 3(2x + 3). Check by expanding: 3(2x + 3) = 6x + 9.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This is about taking out a common factor.","Find the highest number that divides exactly into both terms, and write it outside a bracket.","6 = 3 × 2 and 9 = 3 × 3"],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"Simplify x⁵ × x³.","a":"x⁸","sol":"When multiplying powers of the same base, add the indices: x⁵ × x³ = x⁵⁺³ = x⁸.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This is about the laws of indices for multiplying powers of the same letter.","When you multiply powers of the same base, add the indices together instead of multiplying them.","x⁵ × x³ = x⁵⁺³"],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"Make x the subject of y = 3x − 7.","a":"x = (y + 7)/3","sol":"Add 7 to both sides to remove the constant term: y + 7 = 3x. Divide both sides by 3: x = (y + 7)/3.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about which operations were done to x to build the right-hand side, and in what order.","Undo those operations on both sides, working in the reverse order to how they were applied to x.","y + 7 = 3x"],"codes":["A5"]},{"q":"Expand and simplify 4(3x − 2) − 2(x − 5).","a":"10x + 2","sol":"Expand each bracket separately, taking care with signs: 4(3x − 2) = 12x − 8 and −2(x − 5) = −2x + 10. Combine and collect like terms: 12x − 8 − 2x + 10 = 10x + 2.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This combines multiplying a term over a bracket with collecting like terms.","Expand each bracket separately first, keeping track of each term's sign, then collect the like terms.","4(3x − 2) = 12x − 8"],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"Factorise x² − 16 fully.","a":"(x + 4)(x − 4)","sol":"16 is a square number (4²), so x² − 16 is a difference of two squares: a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b) with a = x and b = 4. This gives x² − 16 = (x + 4)(x − 4).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the structure of this expression — it's a special type of quadratic.","Check whether both terms are square numbers with a subtraction between them; that pattern factorises in a standard way.","16 = 4²"],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"Explain why 2(x + 3) = 2x + 6 is true for every value of x, but 2x + 6 = 10 is only true for one value of x, and state what kind of statement each one is. Find that value of x.","a":"2(x + 3) = 2x + 6 is an identity; 2x + 6 = 10 is an equation, and x = 2","sol":"Expand the bracket in the first statement: 2(x + 3) = 2x + 6, which exactly matches the right-hand side for every value of x, so it is an identity. The second statement, 2x + 6 = 10, is only true for one value of x, so it is an equation. Solve it: 2x = 4, so x = 2.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the difference between a statement that's always true and one that's only true sometimes.","Expand the bracket in the first statement to compare it with the right-hand side; treat the second statement as an equation to solve.","2x + 6 = 10 → 2x = 4"],"codes":["A3","A6"]},{"q":"The formula s = ut + ½at² gives the distance travelled. Find s when u = 5, a = −2 and t = 3.","a":"s = 6","sol":"Substitute the values, working out the power first: s = 5 × 3 + ½ × (−2) × 3². Work out each part: 5 × 3 = 15 and ½ × (−2) × 9 = −9. Add the two parts: s = 15 + (−9) = 6.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["This is about substituting values into an unfamiliar formula given in the question, not one you need to remember.","Substitute each value in the right place, working out the squared term first, then follow the order of operations.","5 × 3 = 15"],"codes":["A2"]},{"q":"A function machine multiplies the input by 4 and then subtracts 1 to give the output. The output is 19. Find the input.","a":"5","sol":"Reverse the operations, undoing them in the opposite order to how the machine applied them: add 1 to the output, then divide by 4. 19 + 1 = 20, and 20 ÷ 4 = 5, so the input is 5.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["This is about undoing a function's operations to work backwards from the output to the input.","Reverse each operation and apply them in the opposite order to how the function machine used them.","19 + 1 = 20"],"codes":["A7"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Factorise 3x² + 11x + 6.","a":"(3x + 2)(x + 3)","sol":"Multiply the coefficient of x² by the constant term: 3 × 6 = 18, then find two numbers that multiply to 18 and add to 11: 9 and 2. Split the middle term using them: 3x² + 9x + 2x + 6. Factorise in pairs and take out the common bracket: 3x(x + 3) + 2(x + 3) = (3x + 2)(x + 3).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is Higher-tier factorising, where the coefficient in front of x² is not 1.","Multiply the coefficient of x² by the constant term, then find two numbers with that product which add to give the coefficient of x.","3 × 6 = 18"],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Simplify fully: (x² − 4)/(x² + 5x + 6).","a":"(x − 2)/(x + 3)","sol":"Factorise the numerator as a difference of two squares: x² − 4 = (x + 2)(x − 2). Factorise the denominator: x² + 5x + 6 = (x + 2)(x + 3). Cancel the common bracket (x + 2): (x − 2)/(x + 3).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Before you can simplify this fraction, look at what you can do to the top and the bottom separately.","Factorise the numerator and the denominator fully, then look for a bracket that appears in both.","x² − 4 = (x + 2)(x − 2)"],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Prove that the sum of three consecutive integers is always a multiple of 3.","a":"3n + 3 = 3(n + 1) — always a multiple of 3","sol":"Let the first integer be n; the next two consecutive integers are n + 1 and n + 2. Add all three: n + (n + 1) + (n + 2) = 3n + 3. Factorise: 3n + 3 = 3(n + 1), which is 3 multiplied by an integer, so the sum of any three consecutive integers is always a multiple of 3.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["This is a general proof, so you need to represent 'any three consecutive integers' algebraically, not just try an example.","Let the first integer be n, write the next two in terms of n, and add all three together.","n + (n + 1) + (n + 2)"],"codes":["A6"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) f(x) = 3x + 2. Find f⁻¹(x).","a":"f⁻¹(x) = (x − 2)/3","sol":"Write y = 3x + 2 and rearrange to make x the subject: y − 2 = 3x, so x = (y − 2)/3. Swap x and y and rename as the inverse function: f⁻¹(x) = (x − 2)/3.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["This is about reversing a function — undoing what f does to its input.","Write y = f(x), then rearrange to make x the subject, treating it like changing the subject of a formula.","y − 2 = 3x"],"codes":["A7"]},{"q":"A rectangle has length (2x + 3) cm and width (x − 1) cm. Find an expression, in its simplest form, for its area.","a":"(2x² + x − 3) cm²","sol":"Write the area as the product of the length and width: (2x + 3)(x − 1). Multiply out the brackets: 2x² − 2x + 3x − 3. Collect the like terms: 2x² + x − 3, so the area is (2x² + x − 3) cm².","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Start from how you find the area of a rectangle, then bring in the algebra.","Write the area as the product of the two given expressions, then expand the brackets fully before simplifying.","2x × x = 2x²"],"codes":["A4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What does a³ mean?","a":"a × a × a."},{"q":"Simplify 4x − x + 3x.","a":"6x."},{"q":"What symbol shows two expressions are identically equal for every value of the letter?","a":"≡ (the identity symbol)."},{"q":"(Higher tier) What does the composite function fg(x) mean?","a":"Apply g to the input first, then apply f to that result."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Algebraic notation","back":"ab means a × b; a² means a × a; a/b means a ÷ b; coefficients are written as fractions, like x/2, not decimals, like 0.5x.","code":"A1"},{"front":"Substituting into a formula","back":"Replace each letter with its given value, putting negative numbers in brackets, then work out the result following the order of operations.","code":"A2"},{"front":"Expression","back":"A collection of terms with no equals sign. It cannot be solved or be true or false — only simplified.","code":"A3"},{"front":"Equation","back":"A statement with an equals sign that is only true for particular values of the unknown letter.","code":"A3"},{"front":"Formula","back":"An equation connecting two or more variables, such as A = πr², used to work out one quantity from others.","code":"A3"},{"front":"Inequality","back":"A statement comparing two expressions using <, >, ≤, ≥ or ≠ instead of an equals sign.","code":"A3"},{"front":"Term and factor","back":"A term is one part of an expression, separated from the rest by + or −. A factor is a number or expression that divides exactly into another.","code":"A3"},{"front":"Laws of indices","back":"aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ; (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ; a⁰ = 1.","code":"A4"},{"front":"Difference of two squares","back":"a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b). Spot it whenever one square term is subtracted from another.","code":"A4"},{"front":"(Higher tier) Factorising ax² + bx + c","back":"Find two numbers that multiply to give a × c and add to give b, split the middle term using them, then factorise in pairs.","code":"A4"},{"front":"Changing the subject","back":"Apply the same inverse operation to both sides of a formula, undoing the operations on the subject in reverse order, until it stands alone.","code":"A5"},{"front":"Identity (≡)","back":"Shown when expanding or simplifying one side gives exactly the other side — true for every value of the letter, not just some.","code":"A6"},{"front":"(Higher tier) Algebraic proof","back":"Represent the general case with letters, such as n, n + 1 and n + 2 for three consecutive integers, then show the required property holds for every case.","code":"A6"},{"front":"Function machine","back":"Takes an input, applies one or more operations to it in order, and produces an output.","code":"A7"},{"front":"(Higher tier) f⁻¹(x) and fg(x)","back":"f⁻¹(x) is the inverse function — it reverses f. fg(x) is a composite function — apply g first, then apply f to the result.","code":"A7"}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Algebraic notation and manipulation — results to know from memory","items":["Laws of indices: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ; (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ; a⁰ = 1. Not in AQA's formulae Appendix — you must know these from memory.","The distributive law, for multiplying a single term over a bracket: a(b + c) = ab + ac. Not given in the exam.","The difference of two squares: a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b). Not in AQA's formulae Appendix — you must recognise and use this from memory.","The identity symbol ≡ shows the two sides are equal for every value of the letter, not just some — unlike the equals sign in an equation. Not given in the exam; this is a notation convention to know.","(Higher tier) Function notation: f(x) is the output of function f for input x; f⁻¹(x) is the inverse function, which reverses f; fg(x) is the composite function, meaning apply g first and then apply f to the result. Not in AQA's formulae Appendix — you must know this notation from memory.","The kinematics formulae v = u + at, s = ut + ½at² and v² = u² + 2as — given in the question when needed; you do not have to memorise them, only substitute into them correctly."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Common mistakes examiners see in this room","items":["Writing a coefficient as a decimal instead of a fraction, e.g. 0.5x instead of x/2 — AQA's notation convention expects the fraction form.","Forgetting brackets when substituting a negative number, so −2² is worked out as −4 instead of (−2)² = 4.","Sign errors when expanding a bracket with a negative multiplier out the front, e.g. writing −2(x − 5) as −2x − 10 instead of −2x + 10.","(Higher tier) When factorising ax² + bx + c, looking for two numbers that multiply to c instead of to a × c.","Leaving an answer only partly simplified, e.g. writing 4x + 12 as 2(2x + 6) instead of 4(x + 3), or a fraction that could still be cancelled — AQA expects work in simplest form even without being told.","(Higher tier) Confusing f⁻¹(x), which reverses f, with 1/f(x), which is a different expression entirely."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-10T20:00:45.374Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Every answer key is correct — all sixteen questions re-solved independently, with each factorisation expanded back out and each rearrangement substituted back in, and the worked examples, check and exit answers, facts and cards check out too, with the (Higher tier) tag correctly placed on all four Higher questions and absent from the foundation ones — so this needs only an editing pass: fix the incoherent \"2(x + 3)\" bullet in the common-mistakes list, tag the ax² + bx + c mistake as Higher, add inequalities to the A3 idea, move surds off the Higher side of A4, and name the kinematics formulae the exam gives in the formulae sheet."}}},"3.2.3":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"3.2.3","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Solving equations and inequalities is the algebra that the rest of the specification leans on: rearranging a formula, finding a missing length, locating where two graphs meet, or checking whether a value fits a constraint all come down to the linear, quadratic, simultaneous and iterative methods taught in this room. Algebra is the largest topic area at Higher tier and about a fifth of the marks at Foundation — 20% of the Foundation assessment and 30% of the Higher — and a 'solve' question, worth several marks for a fully correct method, appears somewhere on every paper, calculator and non-calculator alike.","idea":[{"h":"Linear equations","t":"A17 covers solving a linear equation, where the unknown appears only to the power 1, by applying the same operation to both sides until the letter stands alone: expand any brackets first, collect the unknown terms on one side and the number terms on the other, then divide by the coefficient of the unknown. This includes equations with the unknown on both sides, which is additional foundation content. The command word is 'solve', and AQA's mark schemes reward each correct step towards isolating the letter with a method mark and the final value with an accuracy mark; 'find approximate solutions using a graph' asks you to read off the x-value where a line reaches a given y-value instead — for example, reading the solution of 2x + 1 = 7 from the graph of y = 2x + 1. AQA's mark schemes typically accept such a reading within a small tolerance, rather than demanding pinpoint accuracy.","code":"A17"},{"h":"Quadratic equations","t":"A18 asks for every value of x that makes a quadratic expression equal to zero. Additional foundation content factorises the quadratic into two brackets and uses the fact that if two brackets multiply to give zero, one of them must be zero, giving two solutions (or a repeated one); solutions can also be read from where a quadratic graph crosses the x-axis. (Higher tier) some equations must first be rearranged so that one side is zero before you can factorise, and where factorising will not work cleanly you complete the square or substitute into the quadratic formula, which AQA's Appendix says you must know from memory — it is never printed on the exam paper. AQA's mark schemes give the method mark for a correctly set-up pair of brackets, a completed square or a correct substitution, and the accuracy marks for both roots; formula answers are often asked for to a stated number of decimal places using a calculator.","code":"A18"},{"h":"Simultaneous equations","t":"A19 finds the pair (or, at Higher tier, pairs) of values that satisfy two equations at once. For two linear equations, additional foundation content, you multiply one or both equations so that a letter has matching coefficients, then add or subtract to eliminate it, solve for the remaining letter and substitute back for the other — or read the coordinates where the two lines cross on a graph. (Higher tier) when one equation is linear and the other quadratic, rearrange the linear equation to make a letter the subject and substitute it into the quadratic equation, which usually gives two solutions and so two pairs of values. AQA's mark schemes give a method mark for a correct elimination or substitution step and accuracy marks for each value; both letters of every pair must be stated, or the answer is incomplete.","code":"A19"},{"h":"Iteration","t":"(Higher tier) A20 is a numerical method for equations that cannot easily be solved exactly. You are given, or must rearrange the equation into, an iterative formula written in suffix notation, xₙ₊₁ = f(xₙ), together with a starting value x₀; substituting x₀ into the formula gives x₁, substituting x₁ gives x₂, and repeating this makes the values converge towards a solution of the original equation. A question often first asks you to 'show that' a solution lies in a given interval, using a sign change in the equation, before asking you to iterate. AQA's mark schemes award a method mark for one correct iteration and accuracy marks for later values and for the final answer, rounded to the accuracy the question states; round only that final answer, since rounding values in between makes the iterations drift from the true solution.","code":"A20"},{"h":"Setting up equations from situations","t":"A21 turns a written situation, often a geometrical one such as a perimeter, an area or a shared total, into algebra: choose a letter for the unknown, write an expression for each quantity described, then use a fact you are given to form an equation, or two simultaneous equations if two unknowns are involved. Solve it using the A17 to A19 methods, then interpret the result in the words of the question, since the value of the letter is rarely the final answer on its own — you may need to substitute it back to find a length, an area or an amount of money. AQA's mark schemes give a method mark for a correctly formed equation even before it is solved, so writing it down earns credit, and a question that asks you to interpret the solution loses marks for a bare number with no unit or context.","code":"A21"},{"h":"Inequalities","t":"A22 solves inequalities the same way as equations, except that multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses the inequality sign. Additional foundation content covers a linear inequality in one letter, solved algebraically and shown on a number line with an open circle where the boundary value itself is not included (< or >) and a closed, filled circle where it is (≤ or ≥), with an arrow showing every other included value. (Higher tier) a quadratic inequality is solved by finding the critical values from the matching equation and sketching the parabola to see which region satisfies it, giving the answer as a range, often in set notation such as {x : a < x < b}; a two-variable inequality is shown as a region on a graph, where AQA's Notes require a dashed boundary line for a strict inequality and a solid line for ≤ or ≥. Leaving the wanted region unshaded and labelling it R is AQA mark-scheme practice, not specification text. AQA's mark schemes give the method mark for correctly rearranging the inequality and separate marks for the boundary and for the correct region or range.","code":"A22"}],"examples":[{"title":"Solve 3(2x − 1) = 4x + 7","steps":["Expand the bracket: 6x − 3 = 4x + 7.","Subtract 4x from both sides: 2x − 3 = 7.","Add 3 to both sides: 2x = 10.","Divide both sides by 2: x = 5."]},{"title":"Solve x² − 5x + 6 = 0 by factorising","steps":["Find two numbers that multiply to 6 and add to −5: −2 and −3.","Write the factorised equation: (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0.","Set each bracket equal to zero: x − 2 = 0 or x − 3 = 0.","Solve each: x = 2 or x = 3."]},{"title":"Solve the simultaneous equations 3x + 2y = 12 and 5x − 2y = 4","steps":["The y-terms have opposite signs and equal size, so add the two equations to eliminate y: 8x = 16.","Divide both sides by 8: x = 2.","Substitute x = 2 into 3x + 2y = 12: 6 + 2y = 12.","Solve for y: 2y = 6, so y = 3."]},{"title":"Solve 2x + 5 < 13 and show the solution on a number line","steps":["Subtract 5 from both sides: 2x < 8.","Divide both sides by 2: x < 4.","Since the inequality is strict, draw an open circle at 4.","Draw an arrow from the circle to the left, showing every value less than 4 is a solution."]},{"title":"(Higher tier) (calculator) Use iteration to solve x³ + x = 7 to 3 decimal places","steps":["Show a root exists between 1 and 2: f(1) = 1³ + 1 − 7 = −5 and f(2) = 2³ + 2 − 7 = 3, a change of sign.","Rearrange as xₙ₊₁ = ³√(7 − xₙ) and substitute x₀ = 1.5: x₁ = ³√5.5 ≈ 1.765.","Substitute x₁ back in: x₂ = ³√(7 − 1.765) ≈ 1.736.","Keep iterating: x₃ ≈ 1.740, x₄ ≈ 1.739, x₅ ≈ 1.739.","The value has stopped changing to 3 decimal places, so x = 1.739."]},{"title":"Sam and Priya share £40 so that Priya gets £6 more than Sam. Form an equation and solve it to find how much Sam gets","steps":["Let Sam's share be s pounds, so Priya's share is s + 6 pounds.","Write an equation for the total: s + (s + 6) = 40.","Simplify: 2s + 6 = 40.","Solve: 2s = 34, so s = 17."]}],"check":[{"q":"Solve 5x − 3 = 2x + 9.","a":"x = 4."},{"q":"Factorise and solve x² + x − 12 = 0.","a":"x = 3 or x = −4."},{"q":"(Higher tier) Write down the quadratic formula for ax² + bx + c = 0.","a":"x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / (2a)."},{"q":"What does an open circle mean on a number line showing an inequality's solution set?","a":"That the boundary value itself is not included — used for a strict inequality, < or >."},{"q":"(Higher tier) For the region y > x − 1, would the boundary line y = x − 1 be drawn dashed or solid?","a":"Dashed, because the inequality is strict (>) and the line itself is not part of the solution."}]},"room":{"facts":["(Higher tier) The quadratic formula: x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / (2a), for ax² + bx + c = 0 with a ≠ 0.","(Higher tier) Completing the square: x² + bx + c ≡ (x + b/2)² + c − (b/2)².","Solving an inequality follows the same steps as solving an equation, except that multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses the inequality sign.","On a number line, a strict inequality (< or >) is shown with an open circle at the boundary value; ≤ or ≥ is shown with a closed, filled-in circle.","(Higher tier) On a graph, a strict inequality (< or >) is drawn with a dashed boundary line and ≤ or ≥ with a solid line; a solution set can be written in set notation, such as {x : a < x < b}."],"questions":[{"q":"Solve 5x + 3 = 18.","a":"x = 3","sol":"Subtract 3 from both sides: 5x = 15. Divide both sides by 5: x = 3.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This is a two-step linear equation — think about which operation was done to x last.","Undo the operations in reverse order: first remove the number term, then remove the coefficient.","5x = 15"],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"Solve x² + 2x − 15 = 0 by factorising.","a":"x = 3 or x = −5","sol":"Find two numbers that multiply to −15 and add to 2: 5 and −3. Write the factorised form: (x + 5)(x − 3) = 0. Since the product is zero, one bracket must be zero: x = −5 or x = 3.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["This quadratic factorises into two brackets.","Look for two numbers that multiply to give the constant term and add to give the coefficient of x, then use the fact that if two brackets multiply to zero, one of them is zero.","Two numbers with product −15 and sum 2 — list the factor pairs of 15."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"Solve the simultaneous equations x + y = 10 and x − y = 4.","a":"x = 7, y = 3","sol":"Add the two equations so the y-terms cancel: 2x = 14, so x = 7. Substitute x = 7 into x + y = 10: y = 3.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Look at what happens to the y-terms if you add the two equations together.","Add the equations to eliminate one letter, solve for the other, then substitute back to find the first.","Adding the equations: 2x = 14"],"codes":["A19"]},{"q":"A taxi charges a £3 fixed fee plus £2 for every mile travelled. A journey costs £15. Form an equation for the number of miles, m, and solve it to find m.","a":"m = 6","sol":"Write the cost as an equation: 2m + 3 = 15. Subtract 3 from both sides: 2m = 12. Divide by 2: m = 6.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about which parts of the cost are fixed and which depend on the number of miles.","Write an expression for the total cost in terms of m, set it equal to £15, and solve the linear equation.","2m + 3 = 15"],"codes":["A21"]},{"q":"Solve the inequality 3x + 2 < 23.","a":"x < 7","sol":"Subtract 2 from both sides: 3x < 21. Divide both sides by 3: x < 7.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This solves the same way as an equation, using inverse operations.","Isolate the term with x by removing the number first, then divide by the coefficient of x.","3x < 21"],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"The graph of y = 2x + 1 is drawn for 0 ≤ x ≤ 5. Use the graph to find the solution of 2x + 1 = 7.","a":"x = 3","sol":"The solution of 2x + 1 = 7 is the x-value where the line y = 2x + 1 reaches y = 7. Reading across from 7 on the y-axis to the line, then down to the x-axis, gives x = 3. Checking algebraically: 2(3) + 1 = 7.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what the solution of an equation means when it is shown as a graph of y = 2x + 1.","The solution is the x-coordinate of the point on the line where y takes the value on the other side of the equation.","Find where y = 7 on the graph."],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"Solve 4(x − 2) = 3x + 5.","a":"x = 13","sol":"Expand the bracket: 4x − 8 = 3x + 5. Subtract 3x from both sides: x − 8 = 5. Add 8 to both sides: x = 13.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Start by dealing with the bracket before you try to collect terms.","Expand the bracket fully, then collect the x-terms on one side and the numbers on the other.","4x − 8 = 3x + 5"],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) (calculator) Solve 3x² − 5x − 1 = 0, giving your solutions to 2 decimal places.","a":"x = 1.85 or x = −0.18","sol":"This does not factorise, so use the quadratic formula with a = 3, b = −5, c = −1: x = (5 ± √(25 + 12)) / 6 = (5 ± √37) / 6. Evaluate both solutions and round to 2 decimal places: x = 1.85 or x = −0.18.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Check whether this factorises easily before choosing your method — it does not, so you need the formula you must know by heart.","Identify a, b and c, then substitute carefully into the quadratic formula, working out the part under the square root first.","b² − 4ac = 25 + 12 = 37"],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Which of the points (0, 0), (3, 2) and (2, 3) lies in the region where y > x − 1 and x + y ≤ 4?","a":"(0, 0)","sol":"Test each point against both inequalities. (0, 0): 0 > −1 ✓ and 0 + 0 = 0 ≤ 4 ✓ — satisfies both. (3, 2): 2 > 3 − 1 = 2 is false, since 2 is not strictly greater than 2 — fails. (2, 3): 3 > 2 − 1 = 1 ✓ but 2 + 3 = 5 ≤ 4 is false — fails. Only (0, 0) satisfies both inequalities, so it is the point in the region.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A point lies in the region only if it satisfies both inequalities at once.","Substitute the x and y coordinates of each point into both inequalities in turn, and reject any point that fails either one.","Testing (3, 2) in y > x − 1: is 2 > 3 − 1, i.e. 2 > 2? That is false, so (3, 2) is not in the region."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"A rectangle has sides (x + 3) cm and (2x − 1) cm. Its perimeter is 34 cm. Form an equation in x, solve it, and find the area of the rectangle.","a":"x = 5; area = 72 cm²","sol":"Write the perimeter as 2[(x + 3) + (2x − 1)] = 34, which simplifies to 6x + 4 = 34. Subtract 4: 6x = 30. Divide by 6: x = 5. Substitute back: the two sides are 8 cm and 9 cm, so the area = 8 × 9 = 72 cm².","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Remember a perimeter is the total distance around all four sides.","Write an expression for the perimeter in terms of x, set it equal to 34, and solve — then use your value of x to find the actual side lengths before working out the area.","2[(x + 3) + (2x − 1)] = 34"],"codes":["A21"]},{"q":"Solve the inequality 3x − 4 ≥ 11 and represent the solution set on a number line.","a":"x ≥ 5","sol":"Add 4 to both sides: 3x ≥ 15. Divide both sides by 3: x ≥ 5. Draw a closed (filled-in) circle at 5, since 5 itself is included, with an arrow extending to the right to show all greater values.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Solve this the same way as an equation, then think about which type of circle the inequality sign needs.","Isolate the x-term first, then divide by its coefficient; before you draw, check whether the inequality sign includes 'or equal to'.","3x ≥ 15"],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Solve x² + 6x + 2 = 0 by completing the square, giving your answers as exact surds.","a":"x = −3 + √7 or x = −3 − √7","sol":"Halve the coefficient of x and write the equation as a completed square: (x + 3)² − 9 + 2 = 0, so (x + 3)² = 7. Take the square root of both sides: x + 3 = ±√7. Subtract 3: x = −3 ± √7.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This quadratic does not factorise, and the question does not ask for the formula, so use the other Higher-tier method.","Halve the coefficient of x to write the left-hand side as a completed square, then isolate that square and take the square root of both sides.","(x + 3)² − 9 + 2 = 0"],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Solve the simultaneous equations y = x + 1 and x² + y² = 25.","a":"(x, y) = (3, 4) or (−4, −3)","sol":"Substitute y = x + 1 into the second equation: x² + (x + 1)² = 25. Expand and simplify: 2x² + 2x − 24 = 0, so x² + x − 12 = 0. Factorise: (x + 4)(x − 3) = 0, giving x = −4 or x = 3. Substitute back into y = x + 1: when x = 3, y = 4; when x = −4, y = −3.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The linear equation is already arranged to make y the subject, which is exactly what you need for this method.","Substitute the expression for y into the quadratic equation, which will leave you with a quadratic in x alone to solve — remember to find both y-values afterwards.","x² + (x + 1)² = 25"],"codes":["A19"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) (calculator) Show that x³ − x − 1 = 0 has a solution between x = 1 and x = 2. Then use the iteration xₙ₊₁ = ³√(xₙ + 1) with x₀ = 1.3 to find this solution to 3 decimal places.","a":"x = 1.325","sol":"Let f(x) = x³ − x − 1. f(1) = −1 and f(2) = 5; since f(x) changes sign, a solution lies between 1 and 2. Iterating from x₀ = 1.3: x₁ = ³√2.3 ≈ 1.3200, x₂ = ³√2.3200 ≈ 1.3238, x₃ = ³√2.3238 ≈ 1.3245, x₄ = ³√2.3245 ≈ 1.3247, x₅ ≈ 1.3247. The values have converged to 3 decimal places, so x = 1.325.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["First you need to show a root exists, before you find it numerically.","Evaluate the expression at each end value to show the sign changes, then substitute each new value from the iteration formula back into itself, repeating until the answer stops changing to the accuracy asked for.","f(1) = 1³ − 1 − 1 = −1"],"codes":["A20"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Solve the inequality x² − 4x − 12 ≤ 0, writing your answer in set notation.","a":"{x : −2 ≤ x ≤ 6}","sol":"Solve the matching equation x² − 4x − 12 = 0 by factorising: (x − 6)(x + 2) = 0, giving critical values x = 6 and x = −2. Since the coefficient of x² is positive, the graph is a parabola opening upwards, which is on or below the x-axis between its roots, so the solution is −2 ≤ x ≤ 6, written in set notation as {x : −2 ≤ x ≤ 6}.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Start from the quadratic equation that this inequality is based on.","Find the critical values by solving the matching equation, then sketch the parabola to see which region satisfies 'less than or equal to zero'.","(x − 6)(x + 2) = 0"],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) (calculator) A ladder 10 m long leans against a wall, reaching a point (x + 2) m up the wall, with its foot (x − 3) m from the wall. Use Pythagoras' theorem to form and solve a quadratic equation, giving x to 3 significant figures.","a":"x ≈ 7.11","sol":"By Pythagoras' theorem: (x + 2)² + (x − 3)² = 10². Expand and simplify: 2x² − 2x + 13 = 100, so 2x² − 2x − 87 = 0. This does not factorise, so use the quadratic formula with a = 2, b = −2, c = −87: x = (2 ± √(4 + 696)) / 4 = (2 ± √700) / 4. Since a length cannot be negative, take the positive root: x ≈ 7.11 (3 s.f.).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The ladder, the wall and the ground form a right-angled triangle.","Write Pythagoras' theorem using the two given expressions and the ladder's length, rearrange so one side is zero, then solve the resulting quadratic — reject any solution that would give a negative length.","(x + 2)² + (x − 3)² = 10²"],"codes":["A18","A21"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Solve 2x − 7 = 11.","a":"x = 9."},{"q":"Solve x² − 49 = 0.","a":"x = 7 or x = −7."},{"q":"Solve 5x + 2 ≤ 27.","a":"x ≤ 5."},{"q":"Two numbers have a sum of 15 and a difference of 3. Form and solve a pair of simultaneous equations to find them.","a":"9 and 6 (x + y = 15, x − y = 3, giving x = 9, y = 6)."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Solving a linear equation with brackets","back":"Expand the brackets first, then collect the unknown terms on one side and the numbers on the other, then divide by the coefficient of the unknown.","code":"A17"},{"front":"Solving an equation with the unknown on both sides","back":"Subtract the smaller unknown term from both sides first so the letter appears on only one side, then solve as usual.","code":"A17"},{"front":"Solving a quadratic by factorising","back":"Factorise the quadratic expression into two brackets, then set each bracket equal to zero, since if two factors multiply to zero, one of them must be zero.","code":"A18"},{"front":"The quadratic formula","back":"(Higher tier) For ax² + bx + c = 0 (a ≠ 0): x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / (2a). Not given in the exam — learn it.","code":"A18"},{"front":"Completing the square","back":"(Higher tier) Write x² + bx + c as (x + b/2)² + c − (b/2)², then solve by taking the square root of both sides.","code":"A18"},{"front":"Solving simultaneous linear equations by elimination","back":"Multiply one or both equations so a letter has matching coefficients, then add or subtract to eliminate it, solve for the remaining letter, and substitute back.","code":"A19"},{"front":"Solving a linear/quadratic simultaneous pair","back":"(Higher tier) Rearrange the linear equation to make a letter the subject, substitute it into the quadratic equation, then solve — this usually gives two pairs of solutions.","code":"A19"},{"front":"Iteration","back":"(Higher tier) Substitute a starting value x₀ into the given formula xₙ₊₁ = f(xₙ) to get x₁, then substitute x₁ to get x₂, and so on; the values converge towards a solution.","code":"A20"},{"front":"Setting up an equation from a situation","back":"Choose a letter for the unknown, write an expression for each quantity described, then use a given fact — an equal length, a fixed total, a stated perimeter — to form an equation.","code":"A21"},{"front":"Solving a linear inequality","back":"Solve it exactly like an equation, except that multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses the inequality sign.","code":"A22"},{"front":"Number-line convention for inequalities","back":"An open circle shows a strict inequality (< or >), where the boundary value is not included; a closed, filled circle shows ≤ or ≥, where it is included.","code":"A22"},{"front":"Solving a quadratic inequality","back":"(Higher tier) Find the critical values by solving the matching quadratic equation, sketch the parabola, and read off the range that satisfies the inequality from the sketch.","code":"A22"},{"front":"Graph convention for a two-variable inequality","back":"(Higher tier) A dashed line shows a strict inequality (< or >), excluded from the region; a solid line shows ≤ or ≥, included in the region.","code":"A22"},{"front":"Set notation for a solution set","back":"(Higher tier) {x : a < x < b} means every value of x between a and b. Two conditions can be combined, e.g. {x : x > 2, x < 7}.","code":"A22"}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Solving equations and inequalities — results to know from memory","items":["(Higher tier) The quadratic formula: x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / (2a), for ax² + bx + c = 0 with a ≠ 0. AQA's formulae Appendix lists this as a result you must know — it is not printed on the exam paper.","(Higher tier) Completing the square: x² + bx + c ≡ (x + b/2)² + c − (b/2)². Not given in the exam — you must be able to derive and apply this identity from memory to solve a quadratic that does not factorise.","The rule for solving inequalities: proceed exactly as for an equation, but reverse the inequality sign if you multiply or divide both sides by a negative number. Not given in the exam — this is a rule to apply correctly, not a value to substitute into.","Number-line convention: an open circle for a strict inequality (< or >), a closed circle for ≤ or ≥, with an arrow showing the included values. Not given in the exam — AQA's Notes for this room require this convention.","(Higher tier) Graph convention: AQA's Notes require a dashed boundary line for a strict inequality (< or >) and a solid line for ≤ or ≥. Not given in the exam — you must apply this yourself. (Leaving the wanted region unshaded and labelling it R is common mark-scheme practice, not a requirement stated in the specification.)","(Higher tier) Set notation: write a solution set as {x : a < x < b}, or {x : x > a} for a one-sided range; combine two separate conditions with a comma, such as {x : x > 2, x < 7}. Not given in the exam — AQA's specification requires the solution set to be given in set notation at Higher tier."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Common mistakes examiners see in this room","items":["Forgetting to reverse the inequality sign when multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number: −2x < 8 gives x > −4, not x < −4.","Giving only one root of a quadratic equation, or only one pair of values for a linear/quadratic simultaneous pair — every solution must be stated, not just the first one found.","Losing the ± sign when substituting into the quadratic formula, or making an arithmetic slip inside the square root — work out b² − 4ac as a separate step before taking its square root.","Rounding the values inside an iteration instead of only the final answer, which makes later iterations drift away from the true solution — carry full accuracy through the working and round only at the end.","Drawing a closed circle for a strict inequality, or a solid line for a strict two-variable inequality, instead of an open circle or dashed line — check whether the inequality sign includes 'or equal to' before drawing.","In a simultaneous-equations question, finding x but forgetting to substitute back to find y, or pairing an x-value from one solution with the y-value from the other."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-10T20:31:35.649Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Mathematically this kit is clean — all fifteen answer keys re-solved independently and confirmed, with every root substituted back into the original equation (both roots of questions[1], [6], [10], [11] and [13], both pairs of questions[11], and the rejected negative root of questions[14] checked against the length), both iterations run from scratch to convergence (questions[12] reaches 1.324718 and examples[5] reaches 1.739203, matching the stated 1.325 and 1.739 including every intermediate value), and the check, exit, facts, cards and common-mistakes items all correct, with \"(Higher tier)\" placed accurately on all six Higher questions and on every Higher fact, card and formulae item and absent from all foundation content; the formulae section also correctly states that the quadratic formula must be known and is never printed — so what is left is an editing pass: correct the false \"largest topic area\" claim in lesson.why, stop attributing the unshaded-region-labelled-R habit to AQA's Notes, pay a mark for the number line in questions[9], and give the room a graph-reading question and an A21 worked example."}}},"3.3":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"3.3","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Ratio, proportion and rates of change is worth 25% of the Foundation tier — tied with Number as the joint highest-weighted strand — and 20% of Higher tier, level with Geometry and second only to Algebra's 30%. It threads through the whole course: percentages, simple interest and compound interest underpin financial-maths questions on every paper, direct and inverse proportion connect straight to the linear graphs and equations met in Algebra, and compound units such as speed, density and pressure recur across Geometry and science-flavoured exam contexts. AQA sets these ideas as short, high-frequency AO1 recall-and-apply questions as well as longer AO2/AO3 problems that ask students to set up and justify a method — best-buy comparisons, reverse-percentage 'original value' problems, growth-and-decay contexts — so fluency here protects marks across all three papers, calculator and non-calculator alike.","idea":[{"h":"Converting between standard and compound units","code":"R1","t":"This is basic foundation content: every student must convert fluently between related standard units — length, area, volume/capacity, mass and time — and between compound units such as speed, rates of pay and prices, working entirely in numbers. Additional foundation content extends this to density and pressure, and to algebraic contexts (for example, writing a speed of x km/h in m/s). The method is always the same: convert each part of the unit separately using the correct conversion factor — for area and volume, remember to square or cube the linear factor, e.g. 1 m² = 10000 cm² — then recombine. AQA's papers say 'convert' or 'change … into'; the mark scheme awards the method mark for using a correct conversion factor even before the final value is reached."},{"h":"Scale factors, scale diagrams and maps","code":"R2","t":"This is basic foundation content, and can be assessed on any of the three papers. A scale of 1 : n means that 1 unit of length on the diagram or map represents n of the same unit in real life. To find a real length, multiply the drawn length by the scale factor n, converting units afterwards if the question asks for a different unit; to find a length on the diagram from a real length, divide by n instead. AQA sets this in geometrical problems as well as maps, asking students to 'find the real distance' or 'find the length on the map'; the mark scheme rewards the correct scale-factor multiplication or division as the method step, with the final value and correct units earning the accuracy mark."},{"h":"Expressing one quantity as a fraction of another","code":"R3","t":"This is basic foundation content: write the first quantity over the second as a fraction, making sure both are in the same units first, then simplify. Unlike simplifying a stand-alone fraction, this fraction can come out greater than 1 when the first quantity is bigger than the second — expressing £150 as a fraction of £120, for instance, gives 150/120 = 5/4, an improper fraction, which is a perfectly acceptable final answer. AQA's papers say 'express … as a fraction of …' or 'what fraction of … is …'; the mark scheme awards the method mark for a correct fraction with consistent units, even before it is simplified, and the accuracy mark for the fraction in its simplest form."},{"h":"Ratio notation and simplifying a ratio","code":"R4","t":"This is basic foundation content: a ratio a : b compares two or more quantities of the same kind, and it is fully simplified once every part has been divided by their highest common factor, leaving no factor in common — the same idea as simplifying a fraction. When a ratio contains decimals or fractions, first multiply every part by the same number to turn them into whole numbers, then simplify as usual, e.g. 0.4 : 1.2 becomes 4 : 12 becomes 1 : 3. AQA's instructions are 'write in the ratio …', 'simplify' or 'write as a ratio of integers in its simplest form'; the mark scheme rewards the fully simplified ratio as the accuracy mark, following a correctly scaled method."},{"h":"Dividing a quantity in a given ratio, and ratio in real contexts","code":"R5","t":"This is basic foundation content. To share an amount in a part : part ratio a : b, add the parts to find the total number of parts, divide the amount by that total to find the value of one part, then multiply by a and by b for each share. A ratio can also be given as part : whole rather than part : part — in squash : total = 3 : 8, for example, the parts are not added, because 3 of the 8 parts are already squash (3/8 of the mixture), with the rest being water. Working the other way, once an amount has been split into two parts, that split can be written back as a ratio: £30 split into £18 and £12 is the ratio 18 : 12, which simplifies to 3 : 2. The same ideas apply to conversion, comparison, scaling, mixing and concentration problems, and to best-buy or better-value comparisons, where two different pack sizes are converted to a common unit price — cost per 100 g, say — before being compared. AQA typically asks students to 'share … in the ratio …' or to 'state which is better value, giving a reason'; the mark scheme rewards finding the value of one part, or each unit price, as the method step, and the correct shares or a justified conclusion as the accuracy mark."},{"h":"Expressing a multiplicative relationship as a ratio or a fraction","code":"R6","t":"This is basic foundation content: as well as comparing two quantities with a ratio, the same relationship can be expressed as a single multiplying factor — dividing one quantity by the other to see how many times bigger or smaller it is. For example, if one length is 4 m and another is 18 m, dividing 18 by 4 shows the second is 4.5 times the first, which is the same comparison as the ratio 4 : 18 = 2 : 9. AQA's wording is typically 'write the relationship between … as a ratio' or 'express … as a fraction/multiple of …'; the mark scheme rewards the correctly formed division or ratio as the method step and the simplified value as the accuracy mark."},{"h":"Proportion as equality of ratios","code":"R7","t":"This is basic foundation content: two ratios are in proportion — equal — exactly when one is a scaled copy of the other, which can be tested by cross-multiplication: a : b = c : d exactly when a × d = b × c. This lets students find a missing value in a proportion statement such as 3 : 5 = x : 40, either by finding the scale factor between corresponding parts or by cross-multiplying to form an equation. AQA asks students to 'find x' given equal ratios, or to check 'whether … are in proportion'; the mark scheme awards the method mark for a correct scale factor or cross-multiplied equation, and the accuracy mark for the solved value."},{"h":"Relating ratios to fractions and to linear functions","code":"R8","t":"This is basic foundation content, linking directly to N11 and R14: a ratio x : y = a : b can be rewritten as the fraction y/x = b/a, and then as the linear function y = (b/a)x — a straight line through the origin whose gradient is that ratio between the two quantities. AQA questions typically give a ratio such as 'the ratio of x to y is 2 : 7' and ask students to write y as a fraction of x, or to write the equation connecting x and y — here, y/x = 7/2, so y = 3.5x; the mark scheme rewards the correct fraction or coefficient as the method step and the equation in the form y = kx as the accuracy mark."},{"h":"Percentages, percentage change and simple interest","code":"R9","t":"This is basic foundation content, so every student is expected to handle it confidently, on calculator and non-calculator papers alike. A percentage is a number of parts per hundred, so r% means r/100, and this fraction, or its decimal equivalent, can be used multiplicatively. To express one quantity as a percentage of another, divide the first by the second and multiply by 100 — 18 as a percentage of 40 is (18 ÷ 40) × 100 = 45% — and the same method lets two quantities be compared by turning each into a percentage of some common total. To increase by r%, multiply by (1 + r/100); to decrease by r%, multiply by (1 − r/100); a result can be more than 100% of the original when it is larger than it. Original-value 'reverse percentage' problems must be solved by dividing the given result by the multiplier, not by reapplying the percentage to it. Simple interest adds the same fixed amount every year, based only on the original amount invested. AQA's wording includes 'express … as a percentage of …', 'compare … using percentages', 'increase/decrease by', 'find the original price' and 'find the simple interest'; the mark scheme rewards the correct multiplier, percentage or interest calculation as the method step."},{"h":"Direct and inverse proportion","code":"R10","t":"This is basic foundation content, including its algebraic and graphical representations. In direct proportion, one quantity increases at the same rate as the other: find the constant multiplier between one known pair of values, k = y ÷ x, and use it to scale to a new value, or use the unitary method — find the value for one unit, then scale. In inverse proportion, one quantity increases as the other decreases, and their product stays constant, so 5 workers taking 12 days at the same overall rate means 8 workers take 60 ÷ 8 days. AQA asks students to solve problems stated as 'y is directly/inversely proportional to x' or set entirely in words; the mark scheme rewards identifying the correct type of proportion and constant as the method step, since using the wrong type earns no credit even with correct arithmetic."},{"h":"Compound units: speed, rates of pay, unit pricing, density and pressure","code":"R11","t":"Speed, rates of pay and unit pricing are basic foundation content; density and pressure are additional foundation, so all students are assessed on them even though only the highest attainers are expected to be fully fluent. Each compound unit is a total divided by what it is spread across or over — speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, pressure = force ÷ area — which can be read directly from the unit's name, since 'metres per second' is metres ÷ seconds. To compare two rates fairly, convert them to the same units first. AQA's wording is 'find the average speed', 'find the density/pressure' or 'which is the better rate of pay'; the mark scheme rewards the correct substitution into the compound-unit relationship as the method step, and the value with correct units as the accuracy mark."},{"h":"Comparing lengths, areas and volumes; links to similarity","code":"R12","t":"Comparing lengths, areas and volumes with ratio notation and scale factors is basic foundation content; extending this to similar shapes, including the trigonometric ratios that stay fixed between similar right-angled triangles, is additional foundation. If two shapes are similar with linear scale factor k, their areas scale by k² and their volumes scale by k³ — cylinders of height 4 cm and 10 cm, for example, have linear scale factor 2 : 5, so their curved surface areas are in the ratio 2² : 5² = 4 : 25. AQA asks students to 'find the ratio of the surface areas' or 'find the volume of the similar solid'; the mark scheme rewards the correct linear scale factor as the first method mark, then squaring or cubing it as a second method mark, before the final accuracy mark."},{"h":"Equations that describe direct and inverse proportion","code":"R13","t":"This idea has no basic foundation content: understanding that 'X is inversely proportional to Y' is equivalent to 'X is proportional to 1/Y', and interpreting a given equation such as y = 5x or y = 12/x to say which type of proportion it shows, is additional foundation, so every student is assessed on it. Finding a missing value from a plain proportion statement in x — 'y is directly/inversely proportional to x, y = … when x = …, find y when x = …' — is not this idea at all: every student solves that with R10's basic-foundation constant-multiplier (direct) or constant-product (inverse) method, without ever needing to write down a formal equation. What is Higher tier only is constructing the formal proportionality equation itself — substituting a known pair of values to find the constant k, then writing the general equation y = kx or y = k/x to use for other values — and this becomes essential once a power of x is involved, such as y ∝ 1/x², where the simple multiplier method no longer applies and the equation has to be built and used explicitly. AQA's Higher papers say 'find the equation connecting … and …' or give one value of a squared or cubed relationship and ask students to find another; the mark scheme rewards forming the equation for k as one method mark and using it correctly as a further method or accuracy mark."},{"h":"Gradient of a straight-line graph as a rate of change","code":"R14","t":"This idea has no basic foundation content — it is additional foundation, so every student is assessed on it. The gradient of a straight-line graph, found as change in y ÷ change in x, is itself the rate of change in context: on a graph of cost against time, the gradient is a price per unit time; on a graph of distance against time, it is a speed. A straight line through the origin shows direct proportion, with the gradient equal to the constant of proportionality; a curve that falls and never touches either axis shows inverse proportion. AQA's command word here is 'interpret' — a numerical gradient alone is not enough; the mark scheme requires the value to be stated in the context of the question, with correct units, for the accuracy mark."},{"h":"Instantaneous and average rates of change on a curve","code":"R15","t":"This idea is Higher tier only — there is no Foundation-tier content in R15 at all. On a curve, the gradient is different at every point, so two related rates of change are defined: the average rate of change between two points, found as the gradient of the chord (straight line) joining them, and the instantaneous rate of change at a single point, found as the gradient of the tangent drawn at that point, or estimated numerically using two values very close together on either side of it. AQA's wording is 'estimate the rate of change at …' or 'find the average rate of change between … and …'; the mark scheme rewards identifying the correct chord or tangent as the method step, then the gradient value — with a tolerance for a graphically read tangent — as the accuracy mark, in the correct units for the context."},{"h":"Growth, decay, compound interest and iterative processes","code":"R16","t":"Setting up, solving and interpreting growth and decay problems, including compound interest, is additional foundation content, so every student is assessed on it; working with general iterative processes is Higher tier only. Repeated percentage growth or decay compounds: multiplying by the same multiplier every period is equivalent to raising that multiplier to the power of the number of periods, giving Total = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ for growth, using −r for decay. A general iterative process applies a rule step by step, often checking after each application whether a threshold has been crossed, rather than solving in a single calculation, since no direct formula exists for a question such as 'after how many years is it worth less than half?'. AQA asks students to 'find the total after n years' or, at Higher tier, to carry out an iteration and interpret the result; the mark scheme rewards the correctly raised multiplier, or evidence of correct repeated calculation, as the method marks."}],"examples":[{"title":"Convert a speed of 45 km/h into m/s","steps":["45 km/h means 45000 m travelled every 3600 seconds, since 1 km = 1000 m and 1 hour = 3600 s.","Divide both numbers by 3600 to convert into metres per second: 45000 ÷ 3600.","45000 ÷ 3600 = 12.5, so 45 km/h = 12.5 m/s."]},{"title":"Share £96 between Amir and Zara in the ratio 5 : 3","steps":["Add the parts of the ratio to find the total number of parts: 5 + 3 = 8.","Divide the total amount by the number of parts to find the value of one part: £96 ÷ 8 = £12.","Multiply £12 by each share of the ratio: Amir gets 5 × £12 = £60 and Zara gets 3 × £12 = £36."]},{"title":"A jacket costs £68 after a 20% reduction in a sale. Find the original price","steps":["A 20% reduction leaves 100% − 20% = 80% of the original price, so £68 represents a multiplier of 0.8 applied to the original.","To reverse a multiplier, divide by it instead of multiplying: original price = £68 ÷ 0.8.","£68 ÷ 0.8 = £85, so the original price was £85."]},{"title":"y is inversely proportional to x. y = 15 when x = 4. Find y when x = 6","steps":["Inverse proportion means y = k/x for some constant k; substitute the known values x = 4 and y = 15 to find k: 15 = k/4.","Solve for k by multiplying both sides by 4: k = 15 × 4 = 60.","Write the full equation using this k: y = 60/x.","Substitute x = 6 into the equation: y = 60/6 = 10."]},{"title":"(Higher tier) Estimate the instantaneous rate of growth of a bamboo shoot at t = 4 weeks","steps":["To estimate the rate of change at t = 4, use two heights either side of it: h = 38.7 cm at t = 3.9 weeks and h = 41.3 cm at t = 4.1 weeks.","Find the change in height between these two close points: 41.3 − 38.7 = 2.6 cm.","Find the change in time between them: 4.1 − 3.9 = 0.2 weeks.","Divide the change in height by the change in time: 2.6 ÷ 0.2 = 13, so the instantaneous rate of growth at t = 4 is approximately 13 cm per week."]},{"title":"(calculator) Find the total value of £2400 invested for 3 years at 3.5% compound interest","steps":["Substitute P = 2400, r = 3.5 and n = 3 into Total = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ: Total = 2400 × (1 + 3.5/100)³.","Work out the multiplier inside the brackets: 1 + 3.5/100 = 1.035, so Total = 2400 × 1.035³.","Cube the multiplier: 1.035³ = 1.108717875.","Multiply: 2400 × 1.108717875 = £2660.92, to the nearest penny."]}],"check":[{"q":"Simplify the ratio 20 : 45.","a":"4 : 9"},{"q":"Find 15% of £60 using a multiplier.","a":"£9"},{"q":"y is directly proportional to x. If y = 10 when x = 2, find y when x = 5.","a":"25"},{"q":"State whether y = 8/x shows direct or inverse proportion.","a":"Inverse proportion"}]},"room":{"facts":["Speed = distance ÷ time; density = mass ÷ volume; pressure = force ÷ area.","To convert a compound unit such as km/h to m/s, convert the distance and the time separately, then combine — equivalent to dividing by 3.6 overall.","A percentage increase of r% is a multiplier of (1 + r/100); a percentage decrease of r% is a multiplier of (1 − r/100).","Simple interest = P × r × n ÷ 100, where P is the amount invested, r is the rate per year as a percentage and n is the number of years.","Compound interest / growth: Total = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, applying the multiplier once for each time period; AQA's Appendix lists this among the results you must know or be able to derive — it is not given in the exam.","Direct proportion: y = kx (y ∝ x). Inverse proportion: y = k/x, equivalently xy = k (y ∝ 1/x).","In a ratio a : b, the two shares of the total are a/(a+b) and b/(a+b); dividing one share by the other instead gives a/b.","If two similar shapes have linear scale factor k, their areas scale by k² and their volumes scale by k³."],"questions":[{"q":"Write the ratio 12 : 30 in its simplest form.","a":"2 : 5","sol":"Find the highest common factor of 12 and 30: it is 6. Divide both parts of the ratio by 6: 12 ÷ 6 = 2 and 30 ÷ 6 = 5, giving 2 : 5, which has no factor left in common.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A ratio is fully simplified when its two numbers share no common factor other than 1, in exactly the same way a fraction is simplified.","Find the highest common factor of both numbers in the ratio, then divide each part by it.","The highest common factor of 12 and 30 is 6."],"codes":["R4"]},{"q":"A path is 6 m wide and a road is 21 m wide. Write the width of the road as a multiple of the width of the path.","a":"3.5","sol":"Divide the road's width by the path's width to see how many times one fits into the other: 21 ÷ 6 = 3.5. So the road is 3.5 times as wide as the path.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A multiplicative relationship asks how many times one quantity is the other, not the gap between them, so this calls for division, not subtraction.","Divide the larger width by the smaller width to find how many times one contains the other.","Road width ÷ path width = 21 ÷ 6."],"codes":["R6"]},{"q":"Express 35 as a fraction of 20, giving your answer in its simplest form.","a":"7/4","sol":"Write 35 over 20 as a fraction: 35/20. Divide both the numerator and denominator by their highest common factor, 5: 35 ÷ 5 = 7 and 20 ÷ 5 = 4, giving 7/4.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Expressing one number as a fraction of another just means writing the first number over the second, even when the result is greater than 1.","Write the fraction 35/20, then simplify by dividing top and bottom by their highest common factor.","35 and 20 share a highest common factor of 5."],"codes":["R3"]},{"q":"A coat costs £45 after a 25% reduction in a sale. What was the original price?","a":"£60","sol":"A 25% reduction leaves 100% − 25% = 75% of the original price, so £45 represents a multiplier of 0.75. To reverse the multiplier, divide instead of multiplying: original price = £45 ÷ 0.75 = £60.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The given price is not the full original price — it is what's left after a percentage has already been taken off, so reversing it needs more than adding the percentage back on.","Work out what percentage of the original price the given amount represents, turn that into a decimal multiplier, then divide by that multiplier.","100% − 25% = 75%."],"codes":["R9"]},{"q":"The ratio of x to y is 2 : 7. Write y as a fraction of x + y.","a":"7/9","sol":"Add the parts of the ratio to find the total number of parts: 2 + 7 = 9, so y is 7 of those 9 parts, giving y as a fraction of x + y equal to 7/9.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A ratio splits a total into a number of equal parts, and each named quantity is a fixed number of those parts.","Add the two numbers in the ratio to find the total number of parts, then compare y's share with that total.","2 + 7 = 9."],"codes":["R8"]},{"q":"A map has a scale of 1 : 20000. A road on the map is 8 cm long. Find the real length of the road in kilometres.","a":"1.6 km","sol":"Multiply the map length by the scale factor: 8 × 20000 = 160000 cm. Convert to kilometres: 160000 cm ÷ 100 = 1600 m, then 1600 ÷ 1000 = 1.6 km.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A scale of 1 : 20000 means every 1 cm on the map stands for 20000 cm in real life, so the map length needs scaling up before any unit conversion.","Multiply the map length by the scale factor to get the real length in centimetres, then convert that into kilometres.","8 × 20000."],"codes":["R2"]},{"q":"Priya invests £650 in a savings account paying 4% simple interest per year. Find the interest earned after 3 years.","a":"£78","sol":"Simple interest = P × r × n ÷ 100. Substitute P = 650, r = 4, n = 3: 650 × 4 × 3 ÷ 100 = £78.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Simple interest is the same fixed amount added every year, always worked out from the original amount invested.","Use simple interest = P × r × n ÷ 100, with P the amount invested, r the percentage rate and n the number of years.","650 × 4 × 3."],"codes":["R9"]},{"q":"The cost, in pounds, of hiring a bike for h hours fits a straight-line graph. When h = 0, the cost is £5; when h = 4, the cost is £17. Interpret the gradient of the line in the context of this hire cost.","a":"£3 per hour (the hourly hire charge)","sol":"Gradient = change in cost ÷ change in hours = (17 − 5) ÷ (4 − 0) = 12 ÷ 4 = 3. The gradient of a cost-against-time graph is the rate of change of cost, so the hourly hire charge is £3.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["On a straight-line graph, the gradient itself is the rate of change — here, the extra cost for each extra hour of hire.","Find the change in cost and the change in hours between the two given points, then divide the change in cost by the change in hours.","Change in cost = 17 − 5."],"codes":["R14"]},{"q":"y is directly proportional to x. y = 18 when x = 6. Find the value of y when x = 10.","a":"30","sol":"Find the constant of proportionality: k = y ÷ x = 18 ÷ 6 = 3, so y = 3x. Substitute x = 10: y = 3 × 10 = 30.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Direct proportion means y is always the same multiple of x, so finding that fixed multiplier first unlocks any other pair of values.","Use the given pair of values to find the constant multiplier k = y ÷ x, then multiply the new x-value by k.","k = 18 ÷ 6."],"codes":["R10"]},{"q":"Cleaning liquid is sold in two sizes: a 500 g tub for £2.10, and an 800 g tub for £3.28. Which tub is better value for money?","a":"The 800 g tub (£0.41 per 100 g, compared with £0.42 per 100 g for the 500 g tub)","sol":"Find the cost per 100 g of each: the 500 g tub costs 210 ÷ 5 = 42p per 100 g; the 800 g tub costs 328 ÷ 8 = 41p per 100 g. 41p is less than 42p, so the 800 g tub is better value.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Comparing two different pack sizes directly isn't fair — both prices need to be converted to the same unit amount first.","Find the cost per 100 g, or per gram, for each tub, then compare those two unit prices.","500 g tub: 210p ÷ 5."],"codes":["R5"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) A cup of tea cools along a curve: its temperature is 82°C at time t = 0 minutes and 34°C at time t = 12 minutes. Find the average rate of change of its temperature over this interval, including its sign.","a":"−4°C per minute","sol":"The average rate of change over an interval is the gradient of the chord joining the two points on the curve: (34 − 82) ÷ (12 − 0) = −48 ÷ 12 = −4°C per minute; the negative sign shows the temperature is falling. Because the cooling follows a curve rather than a straight line, this average rate is different from the instantaneous rate of change at any single moment within the interval.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The average rate of change over an interval is the gradient of the straight line, or chord, joining the start and end points, including its sign.","Divide the change in temperature by the change in time, keeping the order consistent so a falling value comes out negative.","Change in temperature = 34 − 82."],"codes":["R15"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) y is inversely proportional to x². When x = 2, y = 8. Find the value of y when x = 4.","a":"2","sol":"Inverse proportion to x² means y = k/x² for a constant k. Substitute x = 2, y = 8: 8 = k/4, so k = 32. The equation is y = 32/x². Substitute x = 4: y = 32/16 = 2.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["'Inversely proportional to x²' means y is a constant divided by x², not by x itself — write the general form before using the given values.","Substitute the given pair of values into y = k/x² to find k, then use that k with the new x-value.","8 = k ÷ 2²."],"codes":["R13"]},{"q":"The ratio (2x + 1) : (x + 4) is equivalent to 3 : 2. Find the value of x.","a":"x = 10","sol":"Equal ratios cross-multiply: 2(2x + 1) = 3(x + 4). Expand both sides: 4x + 2 = 3x + 12. Collect x-terms on one side: 4x − 3x = 12 − 2, so x = 10.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Two ratios that are equal can be turned into a single equation by cross-multiplying, exactly as with equivalent fractions.","Cross-multiply the two ratios to form an equation, expand the brackets, then collect the x-terms and constants separately to solve.","2(2x + 1) = 3(x + 4)."],"codes":["R7"]},{"q":"A small block of aluminium has a mass of 5.4 g and a volume of 2 cm³. Find its density in kg/m³.","a":"2700 kg/m³","sol":"Find the density in the units given: density = mass ÷ volume = 5.4 ÷ 2 = 2.7 g/cm³. Convert to kg/m³ using 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³, since 1 kg = 1000 g and 1 m³ = 1000000 cm³: 2.7 × 1000 = 2700 kg/m³.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Density in g/cm³ and density in kg/m³ are the same physical quantity in different compound units, so finding the density is only the first step.","Find the density in g/cm³ first using density = mass ÷ volume, then convert to kg/m³ using 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³.","5.4 ÷ 2."],"codes":["R1","R11"]},{"q":"Two similar solids have volumes of 64 cm³ and 216 cm³. Find the ratio of their surface areas, giving your answer in the form 1 : n.","a":"1 : 2.25","sol":"Simplify the volume ratio: 64 : 216 = 8 : 27, dividing by 8. Take the cube root of each part to find the linear scale factor: ∛8 : ∛27 = 2 : 3. Square the linear scale factor for the area ratio: 2² : 3² = 4 : 9. Divide both parts by 4 to write it as 1 : n: 1 : 2.25.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Volumes scale by the cube of the linear scale factor, so getting back to lengths from a volume ratio means undoing a cube, not a square.","Simplify the volume ratio, take the cube root of each part to find the linear scale factor, then square that scale factor to get the area ratio.","64 : 216 simplifies to 8 : 27."],"codes":["R12"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) (calculator) A car bought for £18000 loses 12% of its value each year. After how many whole years is it first worth less than half its original price?","a":"6 years","sol":"Each year the value is multiplied by 1 − 0.12 = 0.88. Half of £18000 is £9000. Multiplying repeatedly: after 4 years the value is 18000 × 0.88⁴ ≈ £10794.52 (still above £9000); after 5 years it is 18000 × 0.88⁵ ≈ £9499.17 (still above £9000); after 6 years it is 18000 × 0.88⁶ ≈ £8359.27, which is below £9000. So the value first drops below half its original price during the 6th year, i.e. after 6 whole years.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["There's no single formula that solves 'when does this drop below half' directly — the multiplier has to be applied one year at a time until the value crosses the target.","Multiply the starting price by the yearly multiplier repeatedly, checking after each whole year whether the value has fallen below half the original price yet.","The multiplier for a 12% yearly loss is 1 − 0.12 = 0.88."],"codes":["R16"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write the ratio 16 : 24 in its simplest form.","a":"2 : 3"},{"q":"Find the density of an object with mass 45 g and volume 15 cm³.","a":"3 g/cm³"},{"q":"A salary of £30000 is increased by 5%. Find the multiplier for this increase.","a":"1.05"},{"q":"y is inversely proportional to x. Does y increase or decrease as x increases?","a":"Decreases"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Converting compound units","code":"R1","back":"Convert each part separately then recombine: for speed, change the distance unit and the time unit before dividing; km/h to m/s is equivalent to dividing by 3.6. For area, square the linear conversion factor; for volume, cube it."},{"front":"Using a map scale","code":"R2","back":"A scale of 1 : n means 1 map unit equals n real units. Multiply a map length by n for the real length; divide a real length by n to go the other way."},{"front":"Simplifying a ratio","code":"R4","back":"Divide every part of the ratio by their highest common factor. If the ratio has decimals or fractions, first multiply every part by the same number to clear them, then simplify to whole numbers."},{"front":"Compound interest formula","code":"R16","back":"Total = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, applying the multiplier once for each year or period. This is additional foundation content, assessed at both tiers. AQA's Appendix says it must be known or derived — it is not given in the exam."},{"front":"Testing for proportion","code":"R7","back":"Two ratios a : b and c : d are in proportion exactly when a × d = b × c, by cross-multiplication. Use the same idea to find a missing value in a proportion statement."},{"front":"Ratio as a linear function","code":"R8","back":"If x : y = a : b, then y = (b/a)x — a straight line through the origin, with gradient b/a. The same ratio can be written as the fraction y/x = b/a."},{"front":"Percentage multipliers","code":"R9","back":"An increase of r% is a multiplier of (1 + r/100); a decrease of r% is (1 − r/100). To find an original value, divide the new amount by the multiplier — never re-apply the percentage."},{"front":"Sharing a ratio, and best value","code":"R5","back":"Share an amount in a ratio: add the parts, divide the amount by the total to find one part, then multiply by each ratio number. For best value, convert each option to a cost per common unit and compare."},{"front":"Direct and inverse proportion by the unitary method","code":"R10","back":"Direct proportion: find the value for one unit, then scale up. Inverse proportion: find the fixed total, such as workers × days, then divide by the new value to find the missing one."},{"front":"Speed, density, pressure","code":"R11","back":"Speed = distance ÷ time. Density = mass ÷ volume. Pressure = force ÷ area. Each compares a total amount with what it is spread across or over — read the formula from the unit's name."},{"front":"Similar shapes: length, area, volume scale factors","code":"R12","back":"If the linear scale factor between two similar shapes is k, their areas are in the ratio k² and their volumes are in the ratio k³. Always find the linear factor first."},{"front":"Direct vs inverse proportion equations","code":"R13","back":"Direct proportion: y = kx (y increases as x increases). Inverse proportion: y = k/x, equivalently xy = k (y decreases as x increases). 'X inversely proportional to Y' means X is proportional to 1/Y."},{"front":"Recognising proportion graphs","code":"R14","back":"Direct proportion gives a straight line through the origin, with gradient equal to the constant of proportionality. Inverse proportion gives a curve that falls steadily and never touches either axis."},{"front":"(Higher tier) Chords and tangents","code":"R15","back":"The gradient of a chord joining two points on a curve gives the average rate of change over that interval. The gradient of a tangent at a single point gives the instantaneous rate of change there."},{"front":"(Higher tier) Iterating a percentage change","code":"R16","back":"Apply the same multiplier repeatedly, one time period at a time, checking the result after each step, until the required condition is met — needed when no single formula gives the answer directly."}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Ratio, proportion and rates of change — results to know from memory","items":["Speed = distance ÷ time (and its rearrangements distance = speed × time, time = distance ÷ speed). Not given in the exam — read it from the unit, e.g. 'miles per hour'.","Density = mass ÷ volume (mass = density × volume, volume = mass ÷ density). Not given in the exam.","Pressure = force ÷ area (force = pressure × area, area = force ÷ pressure). Not given in the exam.","Percentage multiplier: an increase of r% is ×(1 + r/100); a decrease of r% is ×(1 − r/100). Not given in the exam.","Simple interest = P × r × n ÷ 100, where P is the amount invested or borrowed, r is the annual rate as a percentage and n is the number of years. Not given in the exam.","Compound interest / growth and decay: Total = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the starting amount, r is the percentage rate per period (use −r for decay) and n is the number of periods. AQA's Appendix lists this among the formulae students must know or be able to derive — it is not printed in the paper either.","Direct proportion: y = kx, where k is the constant of proportionality (also written y ∝ x). Not given in the exam.","Inverse proportion: y = k/x, equivalently xy = k (also written y ∝ 1/x). Not given in the exam.","Similar shapes: if the linear scale factor is k, areas are in the ratio k² and volumes are in the ratio k³. Not given in the exam.","Gradient of a straight-line graph = change in y ÷ change in x; in context this is a rate of change. Not given in the exam."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Common mix-ups in ratio, proportion and rates of change","items":["To find an original value before a percentage change, divide the new amount by the multiplier. Common errors are re-applying the percentage to the new amount instead, or dividing by 80 and stopping there instead of then multiplying by 100.","In inverse proportion, when one quantity increases the other decreases — check this makes sense in context, e.g. more workers means fewer days, before trusting a calculated answer.","When comparing similar shapes, the linear scale factor must be squared for area and cubed for volume — using the same scale factor for length, area and volume is a common error.","Simple interest pays the same amount every year, based only on the original principal; compound interest pays interest on the interest too, so the yearly amount grows — check which the question asks for.","A ratio a : b written as a fraction can mean two different things: a share of the total is a/(a+b), but a direct comparison of one part with the other is a/b — read the question carefully to see which is wanted.","'X is inversely proportional to Y' means X is proportional to 1/Y, not to −Y; as Y gets bigger, X gets smaller, but neither becomes negative."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-10T21:09:42.779Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Every one of the 16 answer keys is correct and matches its solution (including all the compound-decay intermediates: 0.88⁴ gives £10794.52, 0.88⁵ £9499.17, 0.88⁶ £8359.27, and 1.035³ = 1.108717875 giving £2660.92), hints are properly graded and the formulae sheet correctly records compound interest as know-or-derive and not given — but before publishing, fix the R5 section so it covers part : whole ratios, add express-one-quantity-as-a-percentage to R9 and give the room a reverse-percentage question, and correct the R1 coding on question 14 so unit conversion is actually tested."}}},"3.4.2":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"3.4.2","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Mensuration and calculation is where geometry becomes arithmetic: every shape and solid on the specification has a formula, and this room is the one place they are all brought together and applied with real numbers. Geometry and measures makes up 15% of Foundation marks and 20% of Higher marks, appears on both the non-calculator and calculator papers, and its formulae — some given in the exam, some expected from memory — are tested constantly inside longer, multi-step problems as well as on their own, so knowing which group each formula belongs to, and using the correct one without a picture to check against, is where marks are most often won or lost.","idea":[{"h":"Standard units of measure","t":"G14 is basic foundation content, so every student is examined on it: knowing and converting between standard metric units of length (mm, cm, m, km), area (mm², cm², m²), volume and capacity (mm³, cm³, m³, ml, litres), mass (g, kg) and time, plus money in pounds and pence. Questions use command words such as 'Convert' or 'Calculate', or simply embed the units inside a longer problem, and the mark scheme awards a mark for the correct conversion factor before the mark for the converted value — mixing units without converting first, such as adding centimetres to metres, is one of the most common ways a mark is lost even when the rest of the method is right.","code":"G14"},{"h":"Measuring, scale drawings and bearings","t":"G15 is basic foundation content covering three related skills: measuring a line or angle directly from a diagram or scale drawing, reading a distance from a map using a stated scale, and working with bearings — the eight compass points and three-figure bearings, always measured clockwise from north and always written with three digits, such as 007° rather than 7°. Bearing questions are set with 'Measure', 'Find the bearing of' or 'Draw', and the mark scheme rewards a bearing given to the nearest degree with the leading zeros in place; a common technique tested is finding a back bearing by adding or subtracting 180° from a given bearing.","code":"G15"},{"h":"Areas of triangles, parallelograms and trapezia; volumes of cuboids and prisms","t":"G16 is basic foundation content: know and apply area of a triangle = ½ × base × height, area of a parallelogram = base × height, area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, and volume of a cuboid or other right prism (including a cylinder) = area of cross-section × length. These are tested with 'Calculate' or 'Work out', and AQA's mark scheme gives a method mark for substituting the correct values into the formula — using the perpendicular height, not a sloping side — before the accuracy mark for the evaluated area or volume with the correct unit; a prism question often requires finding the cross-sectional area as a separate first step before multiplying by the length.","code":"G16"},{"h":"Circumference and area of a circle; composite shapes; spheres, pyramids and cones","t":"G17 is basic foundation content for circumference = 2πr = πd and area = πr², perimeters of 2D shapes including circles, and the areas of composite shapes built from rectangles, triangles and circles or parts of circles; a question may ask for the answer in terms of π, which the command word 'Calculate' allows when it says so. Additional foundation content extends this to the surface area and volume of spheres, pyramids, cones and composite solids, including frustums, where the question paper prints the sphere and cone formulae — AQA's Appendix lists them as formulae students are not expected to memorise — and the mark scheme rewards correct substitution before the final accuracy mark; a frustum's volume is found by subtracting the volume of the smaller cone removed from the volume of the full cone.","code":"G17"},{"h":"Arc lengths and sectors of circles","t":"G18, additional foundation content, treats an arc length and a sector area as a fraction of the whole circle: the sector's angle at the centre out of 360° multiplied by the circumference for an arc, or by the area for a sector. This is tested with 'Calculate the length of the arc' or 'Calculate the area of the sector', sometimes leaving π in the answer, and the mark scheme gives a method mark for writing the correct fraction θ/360 before the accuracy mark for the final value; the reverse question, finding the angle from a given arc length or sector area, is also set, rearranging the same relationship.","code":"G18"},{"h":"Similarity: lengths, areas and volumes","t":"G19, additional foundation content, applies congruence and similarity to find a missing length in a similar figure, using the fact that corresponding lengths share one scale factor: multiply the known length by the scale factor found from a corresponding pair of sides. (Higher tier) this extends to areas and volumes — if the linear scale factor is k, area scales by k² and volume scales by k³ — tested with questions such as 'find the ratio of the volumes', where the mark scheme rewards converting between length, area and volume scale factors, using a square root or cube root where the given ratio is for area or volume, as the key method step before the final ratio or value.","code":"G19"},{"h":"Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometric ratios","t":"G20, additional foundation content, applies Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c² and the trigonometric ratios sin A = opposite/hypotenuse, cos A = adjacent/hypotenuse and tan A = opposite/adjacent to find a missing length or angle in a right-angled triangle in two dimensions, tested with 'Calculate' or 'Find', where the mark scheme rewards the correctly set-up equation — the right ratio chosen for the given and required sides, or the correct rearrangement of Pythagoras — before the accuracy mark for the rounded or exact answer. (Higher tier) the same tools are applied in general triangles and in three-dimensional solids, such as finding the length of a diagonal through a cuboid, usually by applying the method twice: once in one triangle to find a length, then again in a second triangle using that length.","code":"G20"},{"h":"Exact values of sin, cos and tan","t":"G21, additional foundation content, requires the exact values of sin θ and cos θ for θ = 0°, 30°, 45°, 60° and 90°, and of tan θ for θ = 0°, 30°, 45° and 60°, without using a calculator — as fractions and surds such as ½, √2/2 and √3/2, not decimal approximations. These are tested on the non-calculator paper with 'Write down the exact value of' or inside a 'Show that' question, and the mark scheme awards the mark only for the exact surd or fraction form; a decimal such as 0.866 written in place of √3/2 does not earn the mark, even though it is numerically close.","code":"G21"},{"h":"(Higher tier) The sine rule and the cosine rule","t":"(Higher tier) G22 is higher-only content: know and apply the sine rule, a/sinA = b/sinB = c/sinC, and the cosine rule, a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, to find unknown lengths and angles in any triangle, not just right-angled ones. Exam questions use 'Calculate' or 'Find', and the mark scheme rewards choosing the correct rule for the information given — the cosine rule when two sides and the included angle, or all three sides, are known; the sine rule when a matching side-and-angle pair is known alongside one more side or angle — before the method mark for correct substitution and the accuracy mark for the final answer, usually rounded to 3 significant figures for a length or 1 decimal place for an angle.","code":"G22"},{"h":"(Higher tier) Area = ½ab sin C","t":"(Higher tier) G23 is higher-only content: know and apply Area = ½ab sin C to calculate the area of any triangle from two sides and the angle between them, and to rearrange the same formula to find a missing side or angle when the area is already known. Questions use 'Calculate the area' or 'Calculate the size of angle', and the mark scheme rewards the correctly substituted formula as the method mark, with the accuracy mark for the evaluated area, or for the angle found after rearranging to isolate sin C and applying inverse sine.","code":"G23"}],"examples":[{"title":"Find the area of a trapezium with parallel sides 8 cm and 12 cm and perpendicular height 5 cm","steps":["Add the two parallel sides together: 8 + 12 = 20.","Halve that total: 20 ÷ 2 = 10.","Multiply by the perpendicular height: 10 × 5 = 50 cm²."]},{"title":"Find the area of a semicircle with diameter 10 cm, giving your answer in terms of π","steps":["Halve the diameter to find the radius: 10 ÷ 2 = 5 cm.","Find the area of the full circle: π × 5² = 25π cm².","Halve that area for the semicircle: 25π ÷ 2 = 12.5π cm²."]},{"title":"Find the area of a sector with radius 6 cm and an angle of 150° at the centre, giving your answer in terms of π","steps":["Write the sector's angle as a fraction of the full turn: 150/360.","Find the area of the full circle: π × 6² = 36π cm².","Multiply the fraction by the full area: 150/360 × 36π = 15π cm²."]},{"title":"Find the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle with the other two sides 5 cm and 12 cm","steps":["Square each of the two shorter sides: 5² = 25 and 12² = 144.","Add the two squares together: 25 + 144 = 169.","Square root the total to find the hypotenuse: √169 = 13 cm."]},{"title":"(Higher tier) (calculator) In triangle ABC, a = 7 cm, b = 9 cm and angle C = 50°. Find the length of side c","steps":["Write the cosine rule using the given angle C: c² = a² + b² − 2ab cos C.","Substitute the values: c² = 7² + 9² − 2 × 7 × 9 × cos 50°.","Work out c², keeping the unrounded value: c² = 49 + 81 − 126cos50° = 49.0087… .","Square root and round only now, at the very end: c = √49.0087… = 7.00 cm (3 s.f.)."]},{"title":"(Higher tier) (calculator) Find the area of a triangle with sides 8 cm and 11 cm and an included angle of 35°","steps":["Write the area formula using the two sides and the angle between them: Area = ½ × 8 × 11 × sin 35°.","Multiply the two side lengths and a half together: ½ × 8 × 11 = 44.","Multiply by sin 35° and round to 3 significant figures: 44 × sin 35° = 25.2 cm²."]}],"check":[{"q":"How many millilitres are there in 2.4 litres?","a":"2400 ml."},{"q":"What is the formula for the area of a trapezium, and what do a, b and h stand for?","a":"Area = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the lengths of the parallel sides and h is the perpendicular height between them."},{"q":"A circle has radius 5 cm. What is its area, in terms of π?","a":"25π cm²."},{"q":"In a right-angled triangle, which side is the hypotenuse?","a":"The longest side, opposite the right angle."},{"q":"Without a calculator, what is the exact value of cos 60°?","a":"½."},{"q":"(Higher tier) When would you use the cosine rule instead of the sine rule?","a":"When you know two sides and the angle between them (SAS), or all three sides (SSS) — the sine rule needs a matching side and its opposite angle."}]},"room":{"facts":["Circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd; area of a circle = πr².","Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c² and the trigonometric ratios sin A = O/H, cos A = A/H, tan A = O/A apply only in a right-angled triangle.","Area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h; volume of a prism = area of cross-section × length.","(Higher tier) In similar figures with linear scale factor k, area scales by k² and volume scales by k³.","(Higher tier) The sine rule a/sinA = b/sinB = c/sinC and the cosine rule a² = b² + c² − 2bc cosA apply to any triangle, not just right-angled ones.","(Higher tier) Area of any triangle = ½ab sinC, using two sides and the angle between them.","Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr and sector area = θ/360 × πr², where θ is the angle at the centre."],"questions":[{"q":"A coach journey takes 2 hours 40 minutes. The coach leaves at 09:55. At what time does it arrive?","a":"12:35","sol":"Add the 2 hours to the departure time first: 09:55 + 2:00 = 11:55. Then add the remaining 40 minutes, carrying over into the next hour once the minutes pass 60: 11:55 + 40 minutes = 12:35.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This is about working with time as a standard unit of measure, using the 24-hour clock.","Add the hours and the minutes separately, and remember that 60 minutes makes the next hour, so you may need to carry over.","09:55 + 2 hours"],"codes":["G14"]},{"q":"Calculate the area of a trapezium with parallel sides 7 cm and 11 cm and a perpendicular height of 6 cm.","a":"54 cm²","sol":"Add the two parallel sides: 7 + 11 = 18. Halve that total: 18 ÷ 2 = 9. Multiply by the perpendicular height: 9 × 6 = 54 cm².","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This uses the area formula for a trapezium.","Add the two parallel sides, halve the total, then multiply by the perpendicular height between them.","7 + 11"],"codes":["G16"]},{"q":"Calculate the circumference of a circle with radius 9 cm, giving your answer in terms of π.","a":"18π cm","sol":"Circumference = 2πr, so substitute r = 9: circumference = 2 × π × 9 = 18π cm.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This uses the circumference formula for a circle, given here as a radius.","Substitute the radius into circumference = 2πr, and leave π in your answer rather than using a decimal.","C = 2πr — and 9 cm is the radius, not the diameter"],"codes":["G17"]},{"q":"A right-angled triangle has a hypotenuse of 17 cm and one of the other two sides is 8 cm. Calculate the length of the third side.","a":"15 cm","sol":"Use Pythagoras' theorem, rearranged to find a shorter side: (third side)² = 17² − 8². Work out the squares and subtract: 289 − 64 = 225. Square root the result to find the length: √225 = 15 cm.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This uses Pythagoras' theorem, but you are finding a shorter side, not the hypotenuse.","Square the hypotenuse and the known side, subtract the known side's square from the hypotenuse's square, then square root the result.","17² − 8²"],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"(calculator) A sector of a circle has radius 5 cm and area 20 cm². Calculate the angle at the centre of the sector, correct to 1 decimal place.","a":"91.7°","sol":"Write the sector area formula and substitute the known values: 20 = θ/360 × π × 5². Simplify the right-hand side: 20 = θ/360 × 25π. Rearrange to make θ the subject, keeping the unrounded value until the end: θ = 20 × 360 ÷ (25π) = 91.7° (1 d.p.).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is the reverse of the usual sector-area question — you are finding the angle, not the area.","Substitute the given radius and area into the sector area formula, then rearrange to make the angle the subject.","20 = θ/360 × π × 5²"],"codes":["G18"]},{"q":"The bearing of point B from point A is 072°. Find the bearing of point A from point B.","a":"252°","sol":"A back bearing is found by adding or subtracting 180° from the given bearing, choosing whichever keeps the result between 0° and 360°. Since 072° is less than 180°, add 180°: 072° + 180° = 252°.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["This is about back bearings — the bearing measured in the opposite direction, between the same two points.","Add or subtract 180° from the given bearing, and give the answer as three figures.","a back bearing differs from the original bearing by 180°"],"codes":["G15"]},{"q":"A triangular prism has a cross-section that is a triangle with base 10 cm and perpendicular height 9 cm, and the prism is 6 cm long. Calculate the volume of the prism.","a":"270 cm³","sol":"Find the area of the triangular cross-section: ½ × 10 × 9 = 45 cm². Multiply by the length of the prism to find the volume: 45 × 6 = 270 cm³.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This combines the area of a triangle with the volume of a prism.","First find the area of the triangular cross-section, then multiply that area by the length of the prism.","½ × 10 × 9"],"codes":["G16"]},{"q":"(calculator) A cone-shaped container has radius 4 cm and perpendicular height 9 cm. Using the formula V = ⅓πr²h, calculate the capacity of the container in litres, correct to 3 significant figures.","a":"0.151 litres","sol":"Substitute into the given formula: V = ⅓ × π × 4² × 9 = 48π cm³. Work out the unrounded volume in cm³: 48π = 150.796… cm³. Convert to litres, since 1000 cm³ = 1 litre, rounding only at the end: 150.796… ÷ 1000 = 0.151 litres (3 s.f.).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["This combines the volume of a solid, given to you as a formula, with converting between units of capacity.","Substitute into the given formula to find the volume in cm³ first, then convert to litres using 1000 cm³ = 1 litre.","V = ⅓ × π × 4² × 9"],"codes":["G17","G14"]},{"q":"A sector of a circle has radius 9 cm and an angle of 40° at the centre. Calculate the length of the arc, giving your answer in terms of π.","a":"2π cm","sol":"Write the angle as a fraction of the full turn: 40/360. Find the circumference of the full circle: 2 × π × 9 = 18π cm. Multiply the fraction by the full circumference: 40/360 × 18π = 2π cm.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["This is about the length of an arc as a fraction of the whole circumference.","Work out the sector's angle as a fraction of 360°, then multiply that fraction by the circumference of the full circle.","40/360"],"codes":["G18"]},{"q":"Two similar triangles have corresponding sides of length 6 cm and 9 cm. Another side of the smaller triangle is 8 cm. Find the length of the side that corresponds to it in the larger triangle.","a":"12 cm","sol":"Find the scale factor from the pair of corresponding sides: 9 ÷ 6 = 1.5. Multiply the known side of the smaller triangle by the scale factor: 8 × 1.5 = 12 cm.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["This is about the scale factor between two similar figures.","Divide one corresponding side by the other to find the scale factor, then multiply the known length by that scale factor.","9 ÷ 6"],"codes":["G19"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) (calculator) In triangle ABC, a = 15 cm, b = 10 cm and angle A = 40°. Use the sine rule to calculate the size of angle B, correct to 1 decimal place.","a":"25.4°","sol":"Write the sine rule linking the two known sides with their opposite angles: a/sinA = b/sinB, so 15/sin40° = 10/sinB. Rearrange to make sin B the subject, keeping the unrounded value: sin B = 10 × sin40° ÷ 15 = 0.42852… . Use inverse sine and round only at the end: B = sin⁻¹(0.42852…) = 25.4° (1 d.p.). This is the only valid solution: b is shorter than a, so B must be smaller than A; the obtuse solution 154.6° is impossible because 40° + 154.6° > 180°.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This uses the sine rule, which links each side with the angle opposite it.","Set up the sine rule with the two given sides and their opposite angles, then rearrange to make sin B the subject before using inverse sine.","15/sin40° = 10/sinB"],"codes":["G22"]},{"q":"(calculator) A ship is 250 m from the foot of a vertical cliff, measured horizontally. The angle of elevation from the ship to the top of the cliff is 18°. Calculate the height of the cliff, correct to 3 significant figures.","a":"81.2 m","sol":"The height, the horizontal distance and the angle of elevation form a right-angled triangle, with the height opposite the 18° angle and the 250 m distance adjacent to it, so use tangent: tan18° = height/250. Rearrange to find the height: height = 250 × tan18°. Calculate and round: height = 81.2 m (3 s.f.).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is a right-angled trigonometry problem set in a real-world context, using an angle of elevation.","Decide which sides are opposite and adjacent to the given angle, then choose the trig ratio that connects them, and rearrange before you calculate.","tan18° = height/250"],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"A circle has radius 4 cm. The radius is increased by 50% to make a larger circle. Calculate the percentage increase in the area of the circle.","a":"125%","sol":"Find the new radius after the increase: 4 × 1.5 = 6 cm. Find the area of each circle: original area = π × 4² = 16π cm², new area = π × 6² = 36π cm². Find the increase as a percentage of the original: (36π − 16π) ÷ 16π × 100 = 125%.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["This combines the area of a circle with a percentage change.","Find the new radius after the percentage increase, then compare the two circles' areas using a percentage change calculation.","4 × 1.5"],"codes":["G17"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) (calculator) A cuboid has length 6 cm, width 4 cm and height 3 cm. Calculate the length of the diagonal running through the inside of the cuboid, from one bottom corner to the opposite top corner. Give your answer correct to 3 significant figures.","a":"7.81 cm","sol":"First find the diagonal across the base, using Pythagoras with the length and width: base diagonal² = 6² + 4² = 52. This base diagonal now forms a right-angled triangle with the height and the diagonal you need, so apply Pythagoras again: diagonal² = 52 + 3² = 61. Square root and round: diagonal = √61 = 7.81 cm (3 s.f.).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["This applies Pythagoras' theorem twice, working in three dimensions.","First find the diagonal across the base of the cuboid, then use that diagonal with the height to find the length you need.","6² + 4²"],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) (calculator) Triangle PQR has PQ = 12 cm, QR = 14 cm and area 63 cm², with angle PQR between the two given sides. Angle PQR is acute. Calculate the size of angle PQR, correct to 1 decimal place.","a":"48.6°","sol":"Substitute the two sides and the area into the area formula: 63 = ½ × 12 × 14 × sin(PQR). Simplify the right-hand side: 63 = 84 × sin(PQR). Rearrange to find sin(PQR): sin(PQR) = 63 ÷ 84 = 0.75. Use inverse sine to find the angle: PQR = sin⁻¹(0.75) = 48.6° (1 d.p.).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["This uses the area formula for a triangle, rearranged to find an angle instead of an area.","Substitute the known sides and the area into the formula, then rearrange to make sine of the angle the subject before using inverse sine.","63 = ½ × 12 × 14 × sin(PQR)"],"codes":["G23"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) Two similar solids have volumes in the ratio 8 : 27. Find the ratio of their surface areas.","a":"4 : 9","sol":"Cube root each part of the volume ratio to find the linear (length) scale factor: ∛8 = 2 and ∛27 = 3, so the length ratio is 2 : 3. Square each part of the length ratio to find the area ratio: 2² : 3² = 4 : 9.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This links volume ratios, length ratios and area ratios in similar solids.","Cube root the volume ratio to find the length ratio, then square that length ratio to find the area ratio.","∛8 = 2"],"codes":["G19"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the volume of a cuboid measuring 4 cm by 5 cm by 6 cm?","a":"120 cm³."},{"q":"Write down the formula for the circumference of a circle in terms of the radius, r.","a":"C = 2πr."},{"q":"(Higher tier) Two similar shapes have a linear scale factor of 3. What is their area scale factor?","a":"9 (that is, 3²)."},{"q":"(Higher tier) Write down the cosine rule for finding side a, using angle A.","a":"a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Standard units of measure","back":"Length: mm, cm, m, km. Area: mm², cm², m². Volume and capacity: mm³, cm³, m³, ml, litres (1 cm³ = 1 ml; 1000 ml = 1 litre). Mass: g, kg. Plus time and money.","code":"G14"},{"front":"Three-figure bearings","back":"Measured clockwise from north, always given as three digits, e.g. 072°, not 72°. The bearing of A from B is the back bearing: add or subtract 180° from the bearing of B from A.","code":"G15"},{"front":"Area of a triangle and a parallelogram","back":"Triangle = ½ × base × height. Parallelogram = base × height. The height must always be the perpendicular distance to the base, not a sloping side.","code":"G16"},{"front":"Area of a trapezium and volume of a prism","back":"Trapezium area = ½(a + b)h, using the parallel sides a and b and the perpendicular height h between them. Prism volume = area of cross-section × length.","code":"G16"},{"front":"Circumference and area of a circle","back":"Circumference = 2πr = πd. Area = πr². r is the radius, d is the diameter, and d = 2r. Both formulae must be known from memory, not given in the exam.","code":"G17"},{"front":"Spheres, cones and pyramids","back":"Cone: curved surface area = πrl, volume = ⅓πr²h. Sphere: surface area = 4πr², volume = ⅓×4πr³. Pyramid volume = ⅓ × base area × height. All printed in the question. Frustum volume = full cone minus removed cone.","code":"G17"},{"front":"Arc length and sector area","back":"Both are a fraction θ/360 of the whole circle, where θ is the angle at the centre. Arc length = θ/360 × πd. Sector area = θ/360 × πr².","code":"G18"},{"front":"Similar figures — corresponding lengths","back":"In similar figures, all corresponding lengths share the same scale factor. Multiply a known length by the scale factor to find the corresponding length in the other figure.","code":"G19"},{"front":"(Higher tier) Similar figures — area and volume ratios","back":"If the linear (length) scale factor between two similar shapes or solids is k, the area scale factor is k², and the volume scale factor is k³.","code":"G19"},{"front":"Pythagoras' theorem","back":"a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse — the longest side, opposite the right angle. Only works in a right-angled triangle.","code":"G20"},{"front":"Trig ratios: SOH CAH TOA","back":"sin A = opposite/hypotenuse. cos A = adjacent/hypotenuse. tan A = opposite/adjacent. Label the sides relative to the marked angle before choosing which ratio to use.","code":"G20"},{"front":"(Higher tier) 3D Pythagoras","back":"To find a diagonal through a cuboid, apply Pythagoras twice: first across the base to find the base diagonal, then again using that diagonal together with the height.","code":"G20"},{"front":"Exact trig values","back":"sin: 0, ½, √2/2, √3/2, 1 for 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90°. cos runs in reverse. tan: 0, √3/3, 1, √3 for 0°, 30°, 45°, 60° (undefined at 90°).","code":"G21"},{"front":"(Higher tier) Sine rule and cosine rule","back":"Sine rule: a/sinA = b/sinB = c/sinC — use with a matching side-angle pair. Cosine rule: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cosA — use with two sides and the included angle, or three sides.","code":"G22"},{"front":"(Higher tier) Area of any triangle","back":"Area = ½ab sinC, where a and b are two sides of a triangle and C is the angle between them, the included angle. Works for any triangle, not only right-angled ones.","code":"G23"}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Mensuration formulae — what you must know, and what the exam gives you","items":["Area of a triangle = ½ × base × height. Not in AQA's formulae Appendix — a basic result to know from memory.","Area of a parallelogram = base × height, using the perpendicular height. Not in the Appendix — know this from memory.","Area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h is the perpendicular height between them. In AQA's Appendix as a formula you must know or be able to derive — not given in the exam.","Volume of a cuboid or other right prism = area of cross-section × length. In the Appendix as know-or-derive — not given in the exam.","Circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd. In AQA's Appendix as a formula you must know — not given in the exam.","Area of a circle = πr². In the Appendix as a formula you must know — not given in the exam.","Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr; sector area = θ/360 × πr², where θ is the angle at the centre. Not listed separately in the Appendix — derived from the circle formulae and applied from memory.","(Higher tier) In similar figures with linear scale factor k: area scale factor = k²; volume scale factor = k³. Not in the Appendix — a result to know from memory.","Pythagoras' theorem: a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse. In the Appendix as a formula you must know — not given in the exam.","The trigonometric ratios in a right-angled triangle: sin A = opposite/hypotenuse, cos A = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan A = opposite/adjacent. In the Appendix as know — not given in the exam.","Exact values: sin 0° = 0, sin 30° = ½, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, sin 90° = 1, with cos running in reverse order; tan 0° = 0, tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3. Not in the Appendix — must be memorised.","(Higher tier) Sine rule: a/sinA = b/sinB = c/sinC. In the Appendix as a formula you must know — not given in the exam.","(Higher tier) Cosine rule: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cosA, which rearranges to cosA = (b² + c² − a²)/2bc to find an angle. In the Appendix as know — not given in the exam.","(Higher tier) Area of any triangle = ½ab sinC, where C is the angle between the two given sides a and b. In the Appendix as know — not given in the exam.","Curved surface area of a cone = πrl, where r is the radius and l is the slant height. Given in the question — not required from memory.","Surface area of a sphere = 4πr². Given in the question — not required from memory.","Volume of a sphere = (4/3)πr³. Given in the question — not required from memory.","Volume of a cone = (1/3)πr²h, where h is the perpendicular height. Given in the question — not required from memory.","Volume of a pyramid = ⅓ × base area × perpendicular height. Not in AQA's Appendix at all — only the cone and sphere formulae are listed as given — but it is printed in the question when it is needed, in the same way.","Curved surface area of a cylinder = 2πrh, where r is the radius and h is the height. Not in the Appendix — printed in the question when needed, for the surface area of a composite solid that includes a cylinder."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Common mistakes in mensuration","items":["Circle formulae use the radius, not the diameter — halve a given diameter before substituting into circumference = 2πr or area = πr², or use the diameter forms directly (C = πd).","Volume always needs cubed units (cm³, m³) and area always needs squared units (cm², m²) — carry the correct unit through every line of working, not just the final answer.","Do not round part-way through a calculation and then use the rounded value in the next step — this can change the final answer enough to lose the accuracy mark; only round the final answer, to the accuracy asked for.","Check the calculator is in degree mode before any trigonometry question — an answer worked out in radian mode is wrong even when every other step is correct.","(Higher tier) Choosing between the sine rule and the cosine rule: use the cosine rule when two sides and the angle between them, or all three sides, are known; use the sine rule when a side and its opposite angle are both known alongside one more piece of information.","(Higher tier) When a ratio of areas or volumes is given for similar figures, take a square root (for an area ratio) or a cube root (for a volume ratio) to get back to the length ratio — do not use the given ratio as the length scale factor directly.","A composite shape or solid is split into parts you already know how to find the area or volume of, then those parts are added or subtracted — state which parts are being combined, and whether they are added or subtracted, as part of the working."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-10T21:03:43.052Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Every one of the sixteen answer keys is right — I re-solved all of them independently, including checking the ambiguous case at questions[10] (b < a forces the acute 25.4°) and questions[14] (131.4° also gives 63 cm², and is excluded only by the stated 'acute'), and all ten key ideas, the tier tags and the formulae section are faithful to the specification — but the room never once asks for the volume or surface area of a sphere, cone or pyramid even though the lesson teaches it, and two solutions round mid-method against the kit's own advice; fix those before publishing."}}}} };
